window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"anouar-brahem-blue-maqams":["Brahem's oud with a classic jazz rhythm section: piano, double bass and drums. The pieces are long and unhurried, with melodies in Arab modes stated by the oud and then opened up through improvisation, the drums brushing and swirling rather than keeping strict time. The marker is the sound of the oud floating over a jazz trio, the first time Brahem had worked with a drummer of this stature.","Recorded for ECM with the bassist Dave Holland, who had played on Thimar, the drummer Jack DeJohnette, who had played with Miles Davis and Keith Jarrett, and the British pianist Django Bates. It reunited Brahem with the American jazz tradition after years of chamber projects, and the title announces the meeting: blues and maqam side by side.","Listen for the moments when the jazz musicians must play around notes that do not exist on a piano. Bates often drops out or plays in the high register to leave room for the oud's microtones, and DeJohnette shapes his cymbals to the long Arab rhythmic cycles. The title's two words name two scale systems that, here, share the same tunes.","Critics welcomed it as a meeting of masters, and it was widely listed among the best jazz albums of its year. Some found it long and lacking in surprises; others heard an autumnal, generous record, a summation of Brahem's career. It strengthened the case that maqam and jazz can meet as equals.","Listen first to DeJohnette's drums, unusual on a Brahem record, and how they change the music's breathing. Then compare Thimar, made with Holland twenty years earlier. For the American side of the meeting, try Kind of Blue, another record built on modes."],"rabih-abou-khalil-blue-camel":["A septet record in which Abou-Khalil's oud leads a band of jazz horns, electric bass and Arab and Indian percussion through his own compositions. The pieces run in long, intricate rhythmic cycles, odd-numbered and lopsided, over which the saxophone and flugelhorn improvise as freely as in any jazz session. The marker is the bright, speaking oud lines answered by the warm, airy flugelhorn.","Abou-Khalil, a Lebanese oud player who left Beirut for Munich during the civil war, recorded it for the German label Enja with American jazz musicians including the saxophonist Charlie Mariano, the trumpeter Kenny Wheeler and the bassist Steve Swallow. It came at a time when European labels were actively documenting meetings between jazz and other traditions.","The rhythms are the key: Abou-Khalil writes melodies over Arab rhythmic modes of nine, ten or more beats, and his percussionists keep those cycles while the jazz players solo over them. Listen for how naturally Mariano, who had long experience of Indian music, threads his alto through the uneven bars.","It was praised in the jazz press as one of the most successful fusions of Arab and jazz traditions, and is still often recommended as the best introduction to Abou-Khalil. Some Arab musicians considered his approach too free with the oud tradition; jazz critics generally valued exactly that freedom.","Count along with the drums and see how quickly you lose your place: the cycles are long. Then just follow the oud's melodies. Compare Brahem's Thimar, a quieter and more introverted meeting of the oud with jazz. Notice how the flugelhorn softens every phrase it touches."],"rabih-abou-khalil-arabian-waltz":["Oud, string quartet, tuba and frame drums: an unusual chamber group playing Abou-Khalil's compositions. The strings provide sweeping, sometimes folkloric textures, the tuba supplies a growling, agile bass line and occasionally solos, and the frame drums hold intricate Arab cycles. The marker is the sound of a tuba playing nimble melodies against an Arab rhythm, both comic and moving.","Recorded for Enja with the Balanescu Quartet, a London string quartet known for crossing into pop and folk, the French tubist Michel Godard and the percussionist Nabil Khaiat. Abou-Khalil, by then established in Germany, had long wanted to write for strings, and the chamber setting drew on the Arab tradition of large string ensembles as well as Western quartet writing.","Listen to the title piece: a waltz in name, but the three-beat lilt is folded into a longer Arab cycle, so the dance keeps tripping over itself in an elegant way. The string quartet is asked to play the sliding, ornamented phrases of Arab violin playing, which is why it sometimes sounds more like a Cairo orchestra than a classical quartet.","Critics praised its originality and the string writing, and it is often counted among his best albums. Some classical listeners found the quartet writing thin; jazz listeners missed the horns. Over time it has come to be seen as one of the most successful attempts to write chamber music from within the Arab tradition.","Start with the title track and listen for the waltz hiding inside the Arab rhythm. Then compare Asmahan's Layali al-Uns fi Vienna, an older Arab waltz, and Ravel's Boléro for another obsessive rhythmic frame. Keep an ear on the tuba, which switches between bass line and lead voice without warning."],"dhafer-youssef-abu-nawas-rhapsody":["A jazz quartet record in honour of the medieval Baghdad poet Abu Nawas, famous for poems of wine and pleasure. Youssef plays oud and sings wordless, high falsetto lines that soar over a piano trio, the drums restless and modern, the piano harmonies dense. The marker is Youssef's voice: an extraordinarily high, pure falsetto that floats above the band like a muezzin's call.","Youssef, born in a coastal village in Tunisia, trained as a boy in Quranic chanting before turning to the oud and to jazz in Vienna. Here he worked with the Armenian pianist Tigran Hamasyan, the bassist Chris Jennings and the drummer Mark Guiliana, three younger players who were changing the sound of jazz rhythm sections in the 2010s.","Listen for how the vocal lines, though wordless, carry the melodic shape of religious recitation: long held notes ornamented at the end, short rising figures, a strong central pitch. Hamasyan brings his own Armenian folk modes, which overlap with Arab ones, so the pianist and singer often meet on shared modal ground.","It was widely praised in the European jazz press as one of his strongest records. Some critics felt the falsetto, so striking at first, could become a mannerism; others saw it as one of the most original voices in contemporary jazz. The album helped bring Hamasyan to wider notice.","Listen first just for the voice, then for Guiliana's drums underneath, very different in energy. For contrast hear Brahem's Blue Maqams, another oud-led jazz quartet, far calmer, and Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan for a voice that also reaches ecstasy through Islamic tradition."],"dhafer-youssef-birds-requiem":["A suite-like album of slow, ritual pieces, part requiem, part flight. Oud, voice and kanun carry the Arab and Turkish colours; a trumpet with electronic halo and a clarinet add long, plaintive lines; piano, bass and drums move softly underneath. The textures are wide and atmospheric, and Youssef's falsetto drifts above. The marker is the mix of misty trumpet and Turkish clarinet over a gently pulsing oud.","Youssef recorded it for Okeh with an international group that included the Norwegian trumpeter Nils Petter Molvær and the Turkish clarinettist Hüsnü Şenlendirici. It followed a period of personal loss, and its title and mood reflect mourning. By then he was one of the best-known Arab jazz musicians in Europe, performing at major festivals.","Listen to the meeting of two very different reed and brass traditions: Şenlendirici's clarinet bends and slides in the Turkish Roman style, while Molvær's trumpet is processed into a breathy, ambient cloud. Youssef places them against the oud so that three ways of ornamenting a melody sound at once.","Critics welcomed it as one of his most ambitious and moving records, and it reached audiences beyond jazz. A few found it slow and too atmospheric; admirers hear a contemporary spiritual music crossing three traditions. It cemented his place in European jazz.","Play it as a whole, as a single extended piece, preferably in the evening. Follow the voice and clarinet. Then compare Garbarek's Officium, a Norwegian saxophone over early sacred music, for a similar meeting of jazz and ritual. Let the silences between phrases register as part of the music."],"ibrahim-maalouf-diasporas":["A debut album that sets the quarter-tone trumpet, able to play Arab modes, in a small electric band of guitars, bass and drums. The pieces move from slow laments to driving, rock-tinged grooves, with the trumpet singing melodies in Arab maqam and then improvising. The marker is the trumpet's tone bending onto notes between the Western semitones, a sound that seems to belong to no one tradition.","Maalouf, born in Beirut and raised in France after his family fled the civil war, studied classical trumpet in Paris and played Baroque repertoire before turning to his own music. This was his first album under his own name, made as the French jazz scene was opening to musicians from immigrant backgrounds. The title names his family's experience of scattering.","The trumpet itself is the secret: Maalouf's father, Nassim Maalouf, added a fourth valve to the instrument so it could play quarter tones. Listen for the passages where the melody drops onto one of those in-between notes; it is the sound of an Arab singer's ornament transplanted into brass.","Modest at release, it gained a following in France and began a career that made Maalouf one of the best-selling instrumentalists in the country. Some jazz critics found his later albums too commercial; this debut is often valued as his most personal, rawest statement.","Listen for the quarter-tone moments, which are easy to hear once you know to listen. Then try Kalthoum, his later tribute to Umm Kulthum, to hear the same trumpet in an acoustic jazz quintet. For a classical trumpet comparison, hear any Baroque concerto he once played."],"ibrahim-maalouf-kalthoum":["An acoustic jazz quintet reimagines Umm Kulthum's Alf Leila wa Leila as a continuous suite. The trumpet takes the singer's role, the tenor saxophone and piano weave around it, and the rhythm section moves from swing to Arab rhythmic patterns. Long solos open up the original's sections. The marker is the trumpet phrasing a melody with a singer's pauses and swells, as if breathing her words without saying them.","Released on Impulse!, the historic American jazz label, it featured the pianist Frank Woeste, the saxophonist Mark Turner, the bassist Larry Grenadier and the drummer Clarence Penn, leading New York players. The original song, composed by Baligh Hamdi, was a hit of 1969. Maalouf issued it alongside a very different album, Red & Black Light, to show two sides of his music.","Listen to how the arrangement keeps the structure of the original, the long instrumental introduction and the episodic sections, the way a jazz musician might keep the form of a standard while improvising over it. Maalouf treats Umm Kulthum as a composer of standards, exactly as American jazz treats the Broadway songbook.","It was praised by jazz critics on both sides of the Atlantic and helped introduce Umm Kulthum to a jazz audience. Some Arab listeners found the treatment too free; others welcomed it as a sign of respect from a major jazz label. It is often cited as Maalouf's most serious jazz album.","Listen to the original Alf Leila wa Leila first, at least the opening, then this. Follow the trumpet as the singer and the saxophone as the orchestra. Compare Coltrane's treatment of My Favorite Things for another long jazz meditation on a popular melody."],"mashrou-leila-mashrou-leila":["The debut album of a Beirut band: songs in Lebanese Arabic about love, the city, politics and sexuality, sung with a rich baritone over a mix of indie rock guitars, keyboards and violin. The tunes are catchy and wry, the arrangements jumpy and theatrical. The marker is the electric violin weaving Arab ornaments between guitar riffs, and the singer's dark, expressive voice.","The band formed in 2008 among students at the American University of Beirut, and this first album came out the following year. Lebanon was still recovering from the 2006 war, and young Beirut was hungry for music that spoke about its own life in its own dialect. Singer Hamed Sinno was openly gay, rare in Arab pop, and the band's frankness was part of its appeal.","Listen to the violin, played by Haig Papazian, which often carries the melodic ornaments of Arab classical playing while the rest of the band plays Western indie rock. And notice the use of everyday Beirut slang and Arabic word play, which to local listeners is as important as the music, though we will not quote it here.","It became an instant cult record in Beirut and soon across the Arab world, especially among young urban listeners. Older audiences and conservatives found the subject matter shocking. Critics now regard it as a landmark in Arab independent music, the first album to bring a whole generation's language into rock.","Listen for the violin and the voice together, then for the mix of rhythms. Compare The Smiths for the wry, literate rock song, and Fairuz's Rahbani songs for the Lebanese tradition of witty, urban lyrics the band inherits. Notice how the drums shift between rock beats and Arab patterns."],"mashrou-leila-ibn-el-leil":["Son of the Night: a darker, more electronic album about Beirut nightlife, desire and the emptiness after the party. Synthesizers and drum machines push the songs toward dance music, with violin and guitars woven through, and the singer's baritone sits lower and more brooding. The marker is the throbbing synth bass and four-on-the-floor beat under an Arab-inflected melody.","By 2015 the band was internationally known, touring in Europe and North America, and facing growing hostility at home. In 2016 Jordan cancelled a concert, and in 2017 the waving of a rainbow flag at their Cairo show led to arrests. This album, recorded at the height of their fame, reflects a band confronting both freedom and its dangers.","Listen to how the dance beats are often slightly unsettled, with Arab rhythmic accents layered over the steady club pulse, so the music feels restless rather than euphoric. The album's arc moves through a single night, from going out to coming home, though the songs work alone too.","It was well received by critics in the Arab world and in the international press, which increasingly saw the band as a symbol of Arab LGBTQ visibility and free expression. Some fans missed the rawer earlier sound. The band later went on hiatus amid the pressure and Sinno's move abroad.","Listen at night, in sequence. Then compare the debut to hear how much the sound has changed. Pair with Nancy Ajram's Ah W Noss to hear two different visions of Beirut pop from a decade apart. Listen for where the violin survives inside the electronics, a thread back to the band's first sound."],"omar-souleyman-highway-to-hassake":["A compilation of wedding and party recordings from northeastern Syria: Omar Souleyman's hoarse, urgent voice over furiously fast synthesizer lines and drum machines playing dabke, the region's line dance. The keyboard imitates traditional reed pipes and fiddles with buzzing, bending sounds. The music hardly lets up. The marker is the frantic keyboard solo, a cascade of bent notes at breakneck speed.","Released by the American label Sublime Frequencies, which issued field recordings and cassette-culture music, it collected tracks from Souleyman's huge output of locally sold cassettes, many recorded live at weddings. He had sung in the Jazira region since the 1990s, with the keyboardist Rizan Sa'id and a poet who composed verses on the spot. Few outside Syria had heard of him.","Listen for the voice behind Souleyman's: the poet Mahmoud Harbi often whispers lines to him during performance, and Souleyman sings them back moments later. The keyboard's sound is tuned to Arab and Kurdish modes, quarter tones included, which is why it sounds so alien to Western ears used to synthesizers.","The release caused a stir in Western indie and electronic circles, where Souleyman was taken up as a cult figure. Some critics worried about exoticism and the packaging of Syrian wedding music as Western novelty; others argued it had always been dance music and simply found a new crowd. The debate shaped how such releases are discussed.","Turn it up and do not try to follow it all; dabke is for dancing. Listen for the keyboard solos and the breathless voice. Compare Tinariwen's desert blues for another regional music that crossed to Western audiences in the same years."],"omar-souleyman-wenu-wenu":["Souleyman's first studio album: dabke rebuilt with a cleaner, harder electronic sound. Rizan Sa'id's keyboards race and swirl, the beat thuds like club music, and Souleyman's voice, rough and declamatory, sits right in front. The songs are love songs, short and relentless. The marker is the title track's pulsing, almost hypnotic synth riff, closer to techno than to a village wedding, yet still dabke.","Produced by the British electronic musician Kieran Hebden, known as Four Tet, and recorded in New York, it was released by Ribbon Music. Souleyman had been touring Western festivals since the success of his compilations, and the civil war that broke out in Syria in 2011 had made returning home impossible. The album introduced him to a far wider electronic audience.","Hebden's role is mostly restraint: he leaves the keyboards and voice largely as they were, but sharpens the drums and bass so they hit like a dance-floor record. Listen to the keyboard, which still imitates the mizmar and rababa, the double reed and spike fiddle of rural Syrian dance.","It was praised widely in Western music press and brought Souleyman to major festivals; he also performed at the Nobel Peace Prize concert in 2013. Some critics felt the production smoothed his edges; most saw a respectful collaboration. He remains a symbol of Syrian culture abroad during the long war.","Play the title track loud and listen for how the beat and the keyboard interlock. Then go back to Highway to Hassake to hear the rawer cassette sound, and to Four Tet's Rounds to hear the producer's own world. Notice how little the voice changes; the keyboard carries the drama."],"zeki-muren-manolya":["A slow, tender song in the Turkish art-music style, usually credited to Müren himself, about a love compared to a magnolia flower. His voice carries everything: a soft, rounded tone that swells on long held notes and then turns into tiny ornamental curls. Behind him a small ensemble of oud, kanun zither, violins and ney flute doubles the melody in a loose, shimmering unison, with a light hand-drum keeping the gentle pulse.","By the 1970s Zeki Müren was the most loved singer in Turkey, known as the Sun of Art, a performer who filled nightclubs and casinos and later concert halls. He had trained in the classical Ottoman song tradition, built on the makam system of melodic modes, and he carried it into the age of the long-playing record, radio and television. His shows, with their sequinned costumes and extended stage runways, were an event in themselves.","Listen to how the instruments answer the voice rather than accompany it. After each sung phrase the kanun or violin repeats a fragment of the line, slightly varied, so the song moves in call and echo. Müren also bends certain notes in ways a piano cannot play, because the makam uses intervals smaller than the Western semitone; those slightly flattened pitches are what give the tune its ache.","Songs like this kept Turkish art music popular at a time when Western pop and the new arabesk style were pulling listeners away. Critics of the time sometimes treated Müren's glamour as kitsch, but singers of every later generation, from classical vocalists to pop stars, cite his diction and breath control as a model. His recordings remain the reference for how this repertoire should be phrased.","Listen first only to the voice and notice how clearly every syllable is shaped, even at the softest volume. Then follow the kanun's bright plucked answers. Compare a nineteenth-century song by Dede Efendi to hear the older tradition Müren grew out of, then a Sezen Aksu ballad to hear where Turkish pop took his phrasing next."],"zeki-muren-elbet-bir-gun-bulusacagiz":["One of the best-loved recordings in Müren's catalogue, a song of separation whose title promises that two parted lovers will surely meet again one day. The melody rises in long arches and falls back in small decorated steps, and Müren holds the climactic notes with a full, steady tone before releasing them into a soft sigh. The accompaniment is the classic fasıl group of plucked and bowed instruments moving with the voice.","Müren made his name on state radio in the early 1950s and by the following decade was recording prolifically for Turkish labels. He came from Bursa, studied at the Istanbul Academy of Fine Arts, and absorbed the art-song repertoire from older masters of the Ottoman tradition. This period saw Turkish art music move from the elite drawing room and the radio studio into nightclubs, films and the record shops of every city.","Notice the short instrumental passages between verses. They are not filler: they restate the melody in the instruments' own register and often move briefly into a neighbouring mode before slipping home, a modulation the singer then confirms. Müren's habit of pronouncing every consonant crisply, even in the most emotional moments, was deliberate and much imitated; he treated Turkish diction as part of the music.","The song became a standard, sung at gatherings and covered by later art-music and pop singers alike, and many listeners know it only through Müren's version. Writers on Turkish music point to recordings like this as the high point of a style that later lost ground to arabesk and pop. Others note that his popularity helped keep the art-music repertoire alive for a mass audience.","Hear it as a single long breath: follow the line from its quiet opening to the high held note and listen for how Müren lets the sound fade rather than end. Then compare Manolya to hear the same voice in a lighter mood, and Abdel Halim Hafez for a kindred Arabic crooner of the same years."],"sezen-aksu-firuze":["The title song of Sezen Aksu's 1982 album, a gentle song addressed to a young girl named Firuze, the Turkish word for turquoise, wishing her well as she grows up. It opens with a lilting melody over soft strings and builds into a warm chorus that rises in a single swoop. Aksu sings it with an intimate, slightly husky tone, close to the microphone, more confiding than performing.","Aksu had been recording since the mid-1970s and by the early 1980s was writing much of her own material, something unusual for a Turkish female singer then. Turkey had just come through the military coup of 1980, and television and radio were tightly controlled. Pop music that was neither Western imitation nor arabesk was still finding its shape, and Aksu, writing songs that sounded Turkish in melody but modern in production, was helping to define it.","Listen to the melody against the chords. Aksu writes tunes that lean on the intervals of Turkish modal music, but the arrangement places them over Western harmony, so certain notes sound gently bittersweet. That blend, folk-like contour inside a pop frame, became her signature. The song also works as a lullaby; its rocking rhythm is closer to a cradle song than a dance track.","Firuze became one of Aksu's best-loved songs and a perennial at family occasions, where parents sing it to their daughters. In retrospect critics see the album as a turning point, the moment Aksu moved from successful singer to the central songwriter of Turkish pop, a role she would hold for decades. Her influence on later writers and singers is very hard to overstate.","Listen first for the way the chorus lifts, then go back and hear how quietly the verse begins. Compare a Zeki Müren song for the older art-song phrasing she inherited, then play her 1991 album Gülümse to hear the mature Aksu sound in full, polished production."],"sezen-aksu-gulumse":["Sezen Aksu's 1991 album, whose title means Smile, gathers upbeat dance-pop, mid-tempo ballads and quietly sad songs into one of the most successful records in Turkish pop history. The sound is glossy early-nineties production, synthesisers and drum programming, but woven through it are strings, Turkish percussion and melodies built on modal turns. The title track is a song of encouragement to someone in grief, sung with restraint over a swaying beat.","By 1991 Aksu was the undisputed queen of Turkish pop and a mentor to a circle of younger songwriters and singers. Turkey's media were about to open up: private radio and television stations appeared in the early 1990s, ending the state broadcaster's monopoly and creating a boom for pop. This album arrived just as that market exploded, and its songs were everywhere for years afterwards.","Listen for how many of the arrangements stop the electronic beat for a moment and leave a single Turkish instrument, a ney flute or a clarinet line, alone in the mix. These small openings remind the listener where the melodies come from. Aksu also hides wry, conversational phrasing in the vocal, speaking lines almost as dialogue, which gives the dance songs a theatrical edge.","The album sold enormously and is often ranked among the greatest Turkish pop records. Some critics at the time found the production too slick, but its songs have aged well and are still covered by younger artists. It cemented Aksu's reputation as a songwriter whose work could be both commercially dominant and emotionally serious, a combination critics continue to praise.","Start with the title song and listen to how the melody stays low and calm, as if the singer is speaking to a friend. Then move to the dance tracks for contrast. Pair it with Firuze for her earlier, plainer sound and with Işık Doğudan Yükselir to hear where she turned next, toward the folk roots under her pop."],"sezen-aksu-isik-dogudan-yukselir":["An album whose title means Light Rises from the East, in which Sezen Aksu sets aside pop production for arrangements of folk songs and folk-style songs drawn from across Anatolia, the Black Sea, the Aegean and the Balkans. Each track carries the sound of its region: the bright bowed kemençe of the Black Sea coast, the long-necked bağlama of central Anatolia, the clarinet of the western towns. Her voice is plainer here, unadorned, closer to a village singer than a pop star.","The mid-1990s in Turkey were years of political violence, especially the conflict in the Kurdish southeast, and of fierce argument about national identity. Aksu made a record that celebrated the many peoples and languages of Anatolia side by side, a quietly political act. She worked with traditional musicians from different regions, and the album belongs to a wider return to roots that swept Turkish popular music in that decade.","Notice that the songs are not all in Turkish and not all from inside Turkey's borders. The album moves between languages and regions on purpose, so that its running order is itself an argument about a shared musical geography. Listen also for the rhythms: several tracks use the uneven, limping metres of Anatolian and Balkan dance, counted in groups of nine or seven.","Critics greeted the album as Aksu's most ambitious project, and it opened the door for other Turkish pop stars to record folk material without seeming old-fashioned. Some nationalist commentators objected to its multilingual vision, which only increased its standing among its admirers. It is now regarded as a landmark of the Turkish roots revival and a model of how a pop star can serve a tradition.","Treat it as a journey and listen in order, noticing how the instruments change as the songs move from region to region. Pick out the Black Sea track by its fast, buzzing kemençe. Then hear Âşık Veysel's Uzun İnce Bir Yoldayım for the minstrel tradition beneath it, and the Düğün ve Cenaze album with Goran Bregović for its Balkan sequel."],"sezen-aksu-dugun-ve-cenaze":["A collaboration between Sezen Aksu and Goran Bregović, the title meaning Wedding and Funeral. Bregović's orchestra supplies the brass, the deep male chorus and the stomping rhythms of his film scores; Aksu wrote Turkish words for many of his melodies and sings them in her own phrasing. The record swings between raucous brass dances and slow laments, the two sides of Balkan ceremonial life its title names.","Bregović had become internationally famous through his scores for Emir Kusturica's films and was touring Europe with his Wedding and Funeral Orchestra. Aksu, after her folk album of 1995, wanted to explore the shared music of the former Ottoman lands. The meeting of a Turkish pop queen and a Sarajevo-born composer, two years after the end of the Bosnian war, carried obvious symbolic weight across the region.","Many melodies here will sound familiar to anyone who knows the Kusturica soundtracks, because they are Bregović's tunes in new clothes. What changes is the vocal line: Aksu reshapes the phrasing to fit Turkish stress patterns, so tunes that were brass riffs become songs. Listen too for the moments when the chorus sings in a language other than hers, a reminder of where the material travelled from.","The album sold well in Turkey and toured widely, and many listeners consider it one of Aksu's finest records. Some reviewers found Bregović's arrangements formulaic, the same brass gestures recycled from his earlier work, and the question of his borrowing from Romani and traditional sources hangs over this project as over his others. Its best tracks still sound joyful and grand.","Begin with one of the fast brass numbers and listen for the moment the whole band drops out and Aksu is left with a single instrument. Then play Bregović's Underground soundtrack to hear the originals, and Fanfare Ciocărlia for the unpolished village brass that both of them draw on."],"baris-manco-daglar-daglar":["A slow, brooding rock song in the voice of someone calling out to the mountains in his loneliness, issued as a two-part single. A heavy, swaying beat and fuzzy electric guitar carry a melody that sounds like an Anatolian folk song, and Manço's deep voice cuts through with long, mournful lines. The guitar answers the singer in figures that imitate the bağlama, the long-necked lute of Turkish folk music.","Turkish rock bands of the late 1960s first copied Western hits, but a competition called the Altın Mikrofon, run by the newspaper Hürriyet, encouraged them to set Turkish folk material to rock instruments. Barış Manço, who had studied art in Belgium, moved between Istanbul and Europe in these years and became one of the leaders of what came to be called Anatolian rock. This single was among his first big hits at home.","Listen to the guitar between verses. Instead of Western blues licks it plays rapid repeated notes and slides between pitches, a direct translation of folk lute technique onto an electric instrument. The song's slow, heavy pulse also keeps a faint lilt of a folk dance rhythm underneath the rock backbeat, which is why it feels both familiar and strange to Western ears.","The record was a huge success and helped persuade a generation of Turkish musicians that rock could speak with a local accent. Western listeners discovered it decades later through reissues and compilations of Anatolian psychedelia, and it is now treated as a classic of the genre. Manço's later career as a television personality sometimes obscured how adventurous his early records were.","Start by following the guitar rather than the voice and listen for the moments where it sounds like a folk lute. Then compare Erkin Koray's Elektronik Türküler for the same idea taken further, and Altın Gün's Gece to hear how a Dutch band revived this sound half a century later."],"baris-manco-donence":["A long, slowly building song recorded with Manço's band Kurtalan Ekspres, about the turning of time and the seasons, the title meaning something like turning point or solstice. It starts quietly, almost spoken, over a soft keyboard figure, then gathers guitars, drums and strings into an epic, rolling climax. Manço's voice, deep and unhurried, gives the whole thing the feeling of a tale told by an elder.","Manço formed Kurtalan Ekspres in the early 1970s and worked with them for the rest of his career. By the early 1980s, after the 1980 military coup, Turkish rock had largely retreated, and Manço adapted by writing songs that mixed progressive rock, synthesisers and folk storytelling, often with a moral or philosophical message. He was becoming a national figure, beloved by children and adults alike.","Notice how the arrangement mirrors the song's subject: the music circles back on the same few chords and the same melodic shape, but each pass is louder and fuller, so the song itself turns like the cycle it describes. Listen near the end for the keyboard and guitar playing interlocking lines that resemble the layered patterns of Turkish folk ensembles.","Dönence has become one of Manço's most revered songs, regularly named among the greatest in Turkish rock and covered by many younger bands. Critics who dismissed his 1980s work as family entertainment have revised that view, hearing in songs like this a serious progressive-rock ambition. It is now often played at memorials and public gatherings in his honour.","Give it its full seven minutes without skipping; the payoff depends on the slow build. Listen for the first entry of the drums and then for each new layer. Compare Pink Floyd's Wish You Were Here for a kindred slow-burning epic, and go back to Dağlar Dağlar to hear how far Manço travelled in a decade."],"erkin-koray-debut-album":["Erkin Koray's first long-playing record, gathering songs he had been releasing as singles, and one of the first full albums of Turkish rock. The sound is raw late-sixties psychedelia: fuzz guitar, rumbling bass, organ and loose drums, with Koray's nasal, intense voice on top. Some tracks are love songs in a Western mould; others turn Turkish melodies into long, droning guitar workouts that point to his later experiments.","Koray had been playing rock and roll in Istanbul since the late 1950s, when he was still at school, and is often called the father of Turkish rock. Through the 1960s he moved from covers to original songs in Turkish, at a time when long hair and electric guitars drew hostility from conservative quarters and occasional violence. In the 45-rpm market of the day, an album was a statement.","Listen to the guitar tone. Koray sought ways to make Western instruments play Turkish intervals, bending strings and using slides, and he was among the first to electrify the saz, or bağlama, adding pickups to the folk lute. On this record you can hear him searching for that sound before he found it fully, with some solos hovering between blues and makam.","Little noticed outside Turkey for decades, the album was rediscovered by collectors in the 2000s and became prized as an early document of non-Western psychedelia. Turkish critics had long honoured Koray as a pioneer, though his uncompromising style kept him from the commercial success of Barış Manço or Cem Karaca. Today he is regarded as the most purely experimental of the Anatolian rock founders.","Listen with headphones for the guitar tone and its odd, bending pitches. Pick the most Turkish-sounding track and compare it with the most Western one. Then play Jimi Hendrix's Axis: Bold as Love for the model he admired, and Elektronik Türküler for the fuller fusion he reached the next year."],"erkin-koray-elektronik-turkuler":["The title means Electronic Folk Songs, and the record does exactly that: traditional Turkish türküs and Koray's own songs in the same style, played on electric instruments with heavy psychedelic arrangements. The sound is dominated by his electrified saz, whose buzzing, metallic tone sits somewhere between a sitar and a fuzz guitar. Rhythms come from folk dances, often in uneven metres, driven by a rock drum kit.","By 1974 the Anatolian rock movement was at its height in Istanbul, with bands competing to fuse folk melodies and rock power. Turkey was politically polarised between left and right, and folk music carried strong associations with rural life and with the left. Koray stayed largely apart from politics, pursuing a sound. Recording technology in Turkish studios was basic, and much of the album's rawness comes from that.","The electric saz is the secret. A saz has frets that divide the octave into more steps than a guitar, so Koray's electrified version could play the true folk intervals with rock volume, something an electric guitar could only fake. Listen for the drone strings ringing under the melody; they create a hypnotic hum that links this record to Indian and Middle Eastern music rather than to Western rock.","The album was admired by Turkish musicians but sold modestly. Its reputation grew enormously with reissues in the 2000s, when Western crate-diggers and labels turned Anatolian psych into a cult. Now it is a cornerstone of that canon, and revival bands such as Altın Gün have drawn openly on the Turkish records of this period.","Listen for the moment the electric saz takes its first solo and notice how its tone differs from a guitar. Count the beats in the dance tracks and you may find odd groupings. Then hear Altın Gün's Gece for the modern revival and Âşık Veysel for the acoustic minstrel tradition these songs come from."],"selda-bagcan-selda":["Selda Bağcan's first album, a set of folk songs and protest songs set to a rough, electric rock backing. Her voice is the centre: strong, hard-edged, full of grit, singing without ornament and with a directness that sounds like a public speech. Behind her, bağlama, fuzz guitar and early synthesiser swirl around dance rhythms, and several songs are adaptations of poems or traditional songs about injustice and the life of the poor.","Mid-1970s Turkey was torn by street battles between left and right, strikes and political killings. Bağcan, who had studied physics, became the leading voice of the left-wing folk-protest movement, singing songs by and about workers, peasants and political prisoners. Rock musicians, probably including members of the group Moğollar, backed her on these sessions, linking the protest-folk scene with Anatolian rock.","Listen to how the electric instruments are used. Rather than playing chords under her, they often double the bağlama's melody line, so the arrangement thickens a folk texture rather than replacing it. Some songs set the words of the great folk poets of the past, the aşıks, whose old verses of protest against landlords and governors took on fresh meaning in the 1970s.","Her songs were hugely popular and politically dangerous: after the 1980 coup she was imprisoned several times and for years was denied a passport. The album reached Western audiences only with a 2006 reissue that made it a cult object among psych collectors, and her songs were sampled by hip-hop producers. In Turkey she remains a symbol of principled resistance.","Listen first for the voice and its refusal to soften anything. Then focus on the guitar and synthesiser and hear how they bend around folk melodies. Compare Victor Jara for another singer whose folk songs became political acts, and Erkin Koray's Elektronik Türküler for the rock side of the same fusion."],"theodorakis-epitaphios":["A cycle of eight songs setting parts of Yannis Ritsos's poem Epitaphios, in which a mother mourns her son, a worker shot during a strike. In the 1960 recording that made it famous, the singer Grigoris Bithikotsis is accompanied by bouzouki player Manolis Chiotis and a small popular-music band. The melodies are simple, singable and grave, cast in the rhythms of Greek popular dances, the slow zeibekiko and the hasapiko.","Ritsos wrote the poem in 1936 after police fired on striking tobacco workers in Thessaloniki. Theodorakis, a classically trained composer studying in Paris, set it in 1958, deciding to use the bouzouki and the language of rebetiko instead of the concert hall. In 1960 two rival recordings appeared, one arranged by Manos Hadjidakis with Nana Mouskouri, polished and orchestral, and Theodorakis's own rawer version.","Listen to the bouzouki's role. Before Epitaphios the instrument belonged to the tavern and to rebetiko, music of the urban underclass, and many in the Greek establishment looked down on it. By giving it great poetry, Theodorakis claimed it for art. The two 1960 versions also present a choice that still divides listeners: Hadjidakis's lyrical European arrangement against Theodorakis's earthy popular one.","The record caused a sensation and set off an argument about whether high poetry belonged with popular instruments; it is often taken as the birth of éntekhno, the art-popular song. The right-wing government disliked Ritsos's communism and Theodorakis's politics, and after the 1967 coup their music was banned. Today the cycle is part of Greek cultural memory.","Start with the Bithikotsis version and listen for the bouzouki introduction to each song, then hear the Hadjidakis version with Mouskouri and decide which you prefer. Go back to Tsitsanis to hear the rebetiko world the bouzouki came from, and on to Axion Esti for Theodorakis's larger setting of Greek poetry."],"theodorakis-axion-esti":["A popular oratorio on Odysseas Elytis's long poem of the same name, about Greece, its landscape, its suffering in war and occupation, and its endurance. Theodorakis combines a symphony orchestra and chorus with a popular singer, originally Grigoris Bithikotsis, a narrator reciting the prose sections, and folk instruments like bouzouki and santouri. Choral passages that sound almost Byzantine alternate with songs in the rhythms of Greek dance.","Elytis published the poem in 1959; he would later receive the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1979. Theodorakis worked on the setting in the early 1960s and premiered it in 1964 in Athens, at a moment when he was also a left-wing member of parliament and a leading political figure. The work aimed to bring the high Greek literary tradition and the music of ordinary people together in one sound.","Listen to the choral writing in the slower sections. Theodorakis borrows the sound of Orthodox church chant, with its drone and its modal melodies, especially in the passages that parallel the poem's liturgical structure, which itself echoes the Byzantine service. The popular songs embedded in this frame then sound like voices from the street breaking into a cathedral.","The premiere was a major event and the songs from it quickly became standards. After the 1967 junta banned his music, Axion Esti became a symbol of resistance and was sung in secret. Some classical critics found the mixture of idioms uneven, but in Greece it is regarded as one of the central works of the twentieth century.","Begin with one of the popular songs sung by Bithikotsis, then listen to the full work to hear how they sit within the choral and orchestral frame. Notice the moment the bouzouki emerges from the orchestra. Compare the Byzantine Akathistos hymn for the church sound behind it and Stravinsky's Les noces for another composer setting folk ritual for art forces."],"theodorakis-zorba-the-greek":["Theodorakis's music for Michael Cacoyannis's film of Nikos Kazantzakis's novel, best known for the dance at its end, often called the sirtaki. That piece begins with a slow, stately bouzouki melody over a steady beat and accelerates, phrase by phrase, until it becomes a whirling, breathless dance. Around it the score sets Cretan-flavoured folk textures, laments and themes for the characters, all played by a small popular band and orchestra.","Cacoyannis shot the film on Crete with Anthony Quinn as Zorba and Alan Bates as the bookish Englishman he befriends. Theodorakis, already famous in Greece for Epitaphios, was asked for a score that would sound authentically Greek to a world audience. The final dance was created for the film, and its steps, though inspired by the old hasapiko, were essentially new; many tourists later took them for an ancient tradition.","Listen to the acceleration. The dance tune repeats the same simple melody over and over while the tempo climbs, so the excitement comes almost entirely from speed and the gradual addition of instruments. That is a trick taken from real Greek and Balkan dance, where musicians push the tempo as dancers tire. It is also why the piece works without any harmonic change.","The film was an international success and the dance became the most famous piece of Greek music in the world, played at weddings, in tavernas and at sports stadiums. Purists have objected that it fixed a tourist cliché of Greek culture. Theodorakis himself was ambivalent about its fame eclipsing his serious work, but its craftsmanship is real.","Listen to the final dance from the very quiet start and try to notice the precise moment it begins to speed up. Then hear the other cues, which are less known and more varied. Compare Hadjidakis's Never on Sunday for the other great Greek film song of the era, and Morricone's westerns for how film music made a regional sound global."],"maria-farantouri-mauthausen":["Maria Farantouri's recording of the four songs Mikis Theodorakis wrote on poems by Iakovos Kambanellis, a survivor of the Mauthausen concentration camp. The songs speak of love and loss inside and after the camp. Farantouri's deep, dark contralto, steady and almost without vibrato, carries melodies of great simplicity over bouzouki and a small band, and the restraint of the performance gives the subject its weight.","Kambanellis, later one of Greece's leading playwrights, had been imprisoned at Mauthausen during the war and wrote the poems from that memory. Theodorakis set them in 1965, and Farantouri, then still a teenager, became their voice; she would be his principal singer for decades. The cycle appeared just before the 1967 colonels' coup, after which Theodorakis was arrested and his music banned.","Listen for how little the singer does. Farantouri barely ornaments, rarely rises in volume, and lets the melody speak; the effect is closer to a chant than a pop song. The first song, often called Song of Songs, borrows the title of the biblical love poem, an ironic and moving choice for a song set among the camp's horrors.","The cycle has become one of the best-known musical memorials of the Holocaust and has been sung in many languages, including Hebrew and German, at commemorations across Europe. Critics consider Farantouri's performance definitive. Some listeners initially found her style too severe for popular song, but that severity is now heard as its strength and integrity.","Listen in a quiet room and follow only the voice through the first song, noticing how it stays low and level. Then compare Theodorakis's Epitaphios, sung by a man in a tavern style, to hear how different his music sounds in a woman's dark voice. Selda Bağcan offers a Turkish contemporary who also sang against repression."],"hadjidakis-never-on-sunday":["The song Melina Mercouri sings in Jules Dassin's film Never on Sunday, its Greek title meaning The Children of Piraeus, about the joy of living in the port city. It is a cheerful, light melody in a swinging dance rhythm, with a bouzouki playing bright runs between the phrases. The tune is short and repetitive, built to be sung by anyone, and its open, sunny character is unmistakable within the first few seconds.","Dassin, an American director blacklisted in Hollywood, made the film in Greece with Mercouri as a free-spirited Piraeus woman. Hadjidakis, already a respected composer who had championed rebetiko in a famous 1949 lecture, wrote a score drawing on popular bouzouki music. The song won the Academy Award for Best Original Song, the first such honour for Greek music, and turned the bouzouki into an international symbol.","Listen to the bouzouki part, which plays in a style taken from rebetiko but with the darker modes removed, so it sounds bright and tourist-friendly. Hadjidakis knew exactly what he was doing: he admired the real rebetiko and in this song deliberately polished it for a world audience. The original Greek song is quite different from the many English and other versions that followed.","The song became a worldwide hit in dozens of cover versions. Hadjidakis grew uneasy with its fame and with the clichéd image of Greece it helped spread, and in later years he sometimes spoke of it with irony. Critics regard it as a fine piece of light music whose success opened foreign ears to the deeper tradition behind it.","Listen to Mercouri's original in the film soundtrack and notice the bouzouki breaks between lines. Then hear Tsitsanis or Vamvakaris for the real rebetiko Hadjidakis was smoothing out, and Gioconda's Smile to hear how serious and private his own music could be."],"hadjidakis-giocondas-smile":["An instrumental suite of short pieces for a small orchestra, each a portrait of a mood or a woman, unified by Hadjidakis's gentle, melancholy melodies. There is no singing and almost no Greek folk colour; instead, delicate piano lines, sighing strings and soft woodwind solos trace tunes that sound like songs without words. The whole album has the hushed, intimate quality of chamber music heard late at night.","By the mid-1960s Hadjidakis was famous for Never on Sunday and for countless film and theatre scores, and he was increasingly restless with Greek musical life. Gioconda's Smile was a personal project, released internationally, and stands apart from his popular songs. He would soon spend several years living in New York, away from Greece and its politics, which the 1967 coup made harder still.","The title refers to the Mona Lisa, known in Italian as La Gioconda, and the music shares that painting's ambiguity: many melodies hover between major and minor, happy and sad. Listen for short motifs that pass between instruments and return in later pieces, quietly binding what sounds like a loose collection into a single cycle.","The album was admired on release and has grown in reputation, often named by Greek critics as Hadjidakis's finest record and by many listeners as a favourite. It is sometimes compared with French film music and with Satie for its quiet melancholy. Its refusal of obvious Greekness was itself a statement, a contrast to the tourist sound of his own hit.","Play it without distraction, perhaps late in the evening, and follow the piano through the opening piece. Notice the moments where a woodwind takes over the tune. Compare Never on Sunday to hear the public Hadjidakis, and Theodorakis's Axion Esti for a contemporary who took Greek music in the opposite, grand and public direction."],"hadjidakis-o-megalos-erotikos":["A song cycle whose title means The Great Erotic, setting love poetry from across Greek history, from ancient lyric and Euripides through medieval and folk verse to modern poets such as Cavafy, Solomos and Elytis. The singers, Flery Dandonaki and Dimitris Psarianos, alternate over sparse accompaniment centred on piano. The songs are slow, transparent and intensely lyrical, moving between art song and popular song without settling in either.","Hadjidakis made the record during the years of the military junta, partly while living abroad. Choosing to anthologise three thousand years of Greek love poetry was quietly defiant at a time when the colonels promoted a narrow, militarised idea of Greek identity. He took great care over the choice of voices, preferring untrained, pure tones to operatic or popular stars.","Listen to the way the cycle is arranged chronologically through the poetry, so that the music subtly changes character as the centuries pass, though the instrumental sound stays constant. Hadjidakis also writes melodies that follow the natural stress of each text, which in ancient Greek and modern Greek are quite different; the shift of rhythm is the clue to the era.","Many Greek critics regard the album as Hadjidakis's crowning work, a perfect balance of poetic refinement and melodic simplicity. It sold modestly at first and grew into a classic. Its quiet, inward style influenced a whole school of Greek art-song writers, and it is still performed in concert, though the original singers' versions are considered definitive.","Choose one song whose poet you know, perhaps a Cavafy setting, and listen for how the melody serves the words. Then hear the cycle in order. Compare Theodorakis's Epitaphios for a different way of setting Greek poetry, earthy and public, and Fairuz for another singer of art-popular song in the eastern Mediterranean."],"haris-alexiou-ta-tragoudia-tis-haroulas":["Haris Alexiou's album of songs by the composer Manos Loizos, its title meaning Haroula's Songs, Haroula being her affectionate nickname. The music draws on popular Greek dance rhythms, the zeibekiko, hasapiko and tsifteteli, with bouzouki and accordion, but the melodies and lyrics are reflective, urban and modern. Alexiou's voice is warm, slightly smoky, conversational in the verses and powerful in the climaxes.","Greece had returned to democracy in 1974 after seven years of military rule, and the late 1970s saw an outpouring of music that blended the éntekhno tradition with popular laïko styles. Loizos, a left-wing composer who had suffered under the junta, wrote these songs specifically for Alexiou, who was becoming the leading female singer of her generation. He died in 1982, making this one of his last major works.","Listen to the way Loizos's melodies borrow the modes of rebetiko but avoid its typical melodic clichés; the songs sound traditional at first and then turn in unexpected directions. Alexiou often begins a phrase almost speaking and moves into full voice only at its end, a technique that makes the songs feel like confessions overheard rather than performances.","The album was a major success and is now considered a classic of Greek popular music, often listed among the best Greek albums of the post-junta era. It established Alexiou as a singer who could carry serious song alongside popular repertoire, and Loizos's reputation has grown steadily since his early death. Its songs remain staples in Greek music halls.","Begin with a slow zeibekiko and listen to the bouzouki's introduction before the voice enters. Then compare Tsitsanis to hear the older rebetiko these songs grow from, and Hadjidakis's O Megalos Erotikos for a more refined, art-song approach to the same period's ideals."],"saban-bajramovic-a-gypsy-legend":["A collection that introduced Šaban Bajramović to Western listeners, gathering his Romani songs in a Balkan band setting. His voice is the attraction: deep, husky, full of improvised slides and cries, both tender and rough. Behind him accordion, clarinet and violin trade quick, ornamented lines over dance rhythms, some fast and stamping, others slow and swaying, and the songs move between Romani, Serbian and occasional other languages.","Bajramović, born in Niš in southern Serbia in 1936, was already a legend in Yugoslavia for recordings made from the 1960s onward. He began singing seriously while serving a prison sentence on the island of Goli Otok, imposed for desertion from the army. By the 2000s, the world-music market and the success of Balkan brass and Bregović's scores opened Western Europe to his older style.","Listen to how freely Bajramović treats the beat. In the slower songs he lags behind or rushes ahead of the band, stretching some syllables and compressing others, a jazz-like freedom he was proud of; he loved jazz and spoke of it often. In the faster tracks, the accordion and clarinet often play in parallel, ornamenting the same melody differently, creating a shimmering, slightly blurred unison.","Western critics hailed the record as an introduction to one of the great Romani voices, and Bajramović was nicknamed the King of Gypsy music. Several of his songs were taken up in film soundtracks and by other artists, sometimes without credit, a recurring grievance in Romani music. He died in poverty in 2008, which drew attention to how little he had earned from his fame.","Start with a slow song and listen for the way the voice moves around the beat. Then pick a fast dance number and follow the clarinet. Compare Esma Redžepova for a female Romani star of the same generation, and Bregović's Ederlezi for how Romani song entered Balkan film music."],"esma-redzepova-caje-sukarije":["Esma Redžepova's most famous song, sung in Romani, praising the beauty of a young woman. It moves in a quick, bouncing dance rhythm with an accordion and clarinet leading the instrumental line and a hand drum driving the beat. Redžepova's voice is bright, powerful and full of quick ornaments, and the whole band builds a sense of festive joy that keeps rising toward the end.","Redžepova was born in Skopje in 1943 and was discovered as a teenager by the musician Stevo Teodosievski, who became her husband and bandleader. Together they built a career that made her the first Romani star of Yugoslav radio and records, at a time when Romani artists were usually treated as anonymous entertainers. Yugoslav state media under Tito encouraged the performance of minority folk traditions.","Listen to the way the rhythm is felt rather than counted: the dance is in a lilting pattern that Western ears tend to hear as straight but which has a slight lengthening on certain beats. Redžepova's ornaments also follow traditional Romani vocal technique, quick turns and catches in the throat that are almost impossible to notate, which gives the song its shine.","The song became one of the best-known Romani melodies in the world and was later used in the 2006 film Borat without her permission, which led to legal action. Redžepova's reputation grew from local star to international ambassador of Romani music; she was celebrated as the Queen of the Gypsies and represented Macedonia at the Eurovision Song Contest in 2013.","Listen for the moment the clarinet joins the voice and how the tempo seems to push forward. Then compare Šaban Bajramović for the male Romani voice of the same generation and Fanfare Ciocărlia for brass versions of this dance energy. Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances offer a classical composer's response to similar music."],"ivo-papasov-orpheus-ascending":["Ivo Papasov's first internationally released album, recorded with his band Trakiya. The music is Bulgarian wedding music at full throttle: lightning-fast clarinet improvisations over dance rhythms in uneven metres, groups of seven, nine and eleven beats, driven by electric bass and drum kit. Saxophone and accordion join in breakneck unisons, and there are moments of jazz-like freedom amid the folk melodies.","Wedding bands in communist Bulgaria played for days at a time, mixing folk, Turkish, Romani and Western pop elements. The regime disapproved of this hybrid music and, during the 1980s campaign against Bulgaria's Turkish minority, harassed musicians who played Turkish-flavoured styles; Papasov, of Romani-Turkish origin, was briefly imprisoned. This album appeared on Hannibal Records in the year the communist government fell.","Listen to how the rhythms are counted. A tune in eleven might feel like a stumble at first, but the band locks into it with total ease, and Papasov's clarinet phrases often run across the bar lines, as if he were deliberately disguising the beat. The tunes also borrow freely from Turkish modes, a fact politically charged at that time in Bulgaria.","Western critics responded with amazement; the record was compared with the most virtuosic jazz and introduced the term Bulgarian wedding music to Western listeners. Some world-music critics debated whether the Western interest exoticised the music, but Papasov's own technical command made such arguments almost irrelevant. He remains regarded as one of the great clarinettists of any tradition.","Pick one fast track and try to clap along; you will quickly feel the uneven groupings. Then listen to the clarinet improvisations for their blurring speed and their sudden lyrical turns. Compare Bartók's Six Dances in Bulgarian Rhythm for a composer's take on the same metres, and Naftule Brandwein for an older clarinet virtuoso from a neighbouring tradition."],"ivo-papasov-balkanology":["The follow-up to Orpheus Ascending, again with Papasov's band, in a fuller, more polished production. The pieces are longer and more varied: some fast dance tunes in complex metres, some slower improvisations where the clarinet sings in long, ornamented phrases over a drone. Electric guitar and saxophone take more solo space, and the record pushes further toward jazz fusion while keeping wedding-music energy.","Released two years after the fall of communism in Bulgaria, the album came as Papasov was touring Western Europe and America for the first time to enthusiastic audiences. Bulgarian music was briefly fashionable in the West, thanks also to the success of the Mystère des Voix Bulgares choral records. Wedding bands at home were now free to play what they liked, and the style was evolving fast.","Listen in the slow pieces for the free, unmetered introductions where Papasov plays alone or over a single sustained note; these are the taksim tradition of Turkish and Balkan music, an improvised prelude that explores the mode before the dance begins. Notice how he often hints at the coming dance tune near the end of the improvisation.","Critics praised the record for its ambition and polish, though some preferred the rawness of Orpheus Ascending. His reputation as a virtuoso was cemented by the tours that followed, where Western audiences heard the band's speed at first hand. Today both albums are considered essential documents of the Balkan wedding music scene at the moment it reached the world.","Start with a slow track to hear the improvised introduction, then put on a fast one and listen for the moment the full band crashes in. Compare Le Mystère des Voix Bulgares for the other Bulgarian music fashionable at the time, and Mahavishnu Orchestra for jazz-rock virtuosity in unusual metres."],"bijelo-dugme-kad-bi-bio-bijelo-dugme":["Bijelo Dugme's debut album, its title meaning If I Were a White Button, a play on the band's name. The record mixes hard rock in the style of early-seventies British bands with melodies and rhythms drawn from Bosnian and Balkan folk song. Goran Bregović's guitar riffs are loud and blues-based, Željko Bebek's high voice rides above them, and between the rock tracks come ballads with a folk lilt.","Bijelo Dugme formed in Sarajevo and quickly became the biggest band in Yugoslavia. Tito's Yugoslavia was more open to Western culture than other socialist states, and rock had a large youth audience, but local bands had mostly copied foreign models. Bregović's idea of mixing rock with folk, which critics called shepherd rock, gave Yugoslav rock its own voice and made the album a phenomenon.","Listen to the guitar figures in the faster songs: some riffs are built on folk scales rather than blues scales, and certain melodic turns would be at home in a Bosnian village song. That small shift is what the press meant by shepherd rock. Listen too for Bebek's ornaments at the ends of phrases, little catches borrowed from folk singing rather than from Robert Plant.","The album sold in unprecedented numbers for a Yugoslav rock record and launched a decade of Bijelo Dugme dominance. Some rock purists mocked the folk element, but it became the template for much later Yugoslav rock. Today it is regarded as one of the most important debuts in the history of the region's popular music.","Listen first for the loud, riff-driven opener and then for the folk-tinged ballads, noticing how the same band shifts style. Compare Led Zeppelin III for a British band blending hard rock and folk at the same time, and Bregović's later film music to hear what became of his folk instincts."],"bijelo-dugme-bitanga-i-princeza":["An album whose title means The Rascal and the Princess, showing Bijelo Dugme at the height of their power. The sound is fuller than on their debut: big, layered guitars and keyboards, orchestral strings on the ballads, and a more dramatic vocal performance by Željko Bebek. The record alternates hard-rock tracks with sweeping, melancholy ballads that became among the most sung songs in Yugoslavia.","By 1979 the band had survived a pause in activity, probably while Bregović completed his military service, and had returned more popular than ever. Yugoslav rock was diversifying, with punk and new wave rising in Zagreb and Belgrade, and Bijelo Dugme responded with a more polished, ambitious record. The country was nearing the end of the Tito era; he died the following year.","Listen to the ballads for their strings. Bregović was beginning to write for orchestra, and the string arrangements here anticipate the grand, cinematic style of his later film scores. Notice too how some melodies use repeated small phrases that rise step by step, a technique he would use again and again in his scores for Kusturica.","The album was a massive commercial success and is often named one of the greatest Yugoslav rock albums. Some critics in the new-wave scene saw Bijelo Dugme as old-fashioned arena rock, but the ballads proved lasting, and many are still sung across the former Yugoslav states. Its reputation has only grown with nostalgia for a shared musical culture.","Start with one of the ballads and listen for the moment the strings enter. Then hear a heavy track to compare the two faces of the band. Compare Bregović's Underground for how these orchestral instincts later grew, and Pink Floyd's The Wall from the same year for another epic, string-laden rock statement."],"bijelo-dugme-pljuni-i-zapjevaj":["An album whose title means Spit and Sing, My Yugoslavia, a deliberately pan-Yugoslav record mixing rock, folk melodies and Romani songs. Singer Alen Islamović leads a sound that blends synthesisers and rock rhythm with brass, folk instruments and choruses. The best-known track, Đurđevdan, is a Romani song for St George's Day, set to the same traditional melody that Bregović later arranged as Ederlezi.","By the mid-1980s Yugoslavia was in political and economic crisis after Tito's death, with nationalist tensions growing among its republics. Bregović designed the album as a plea for unity, drawing on music from across the country's peoples. The band recorded in this spirit of national togetherness just a few years before the federation collapsed into war in the early 1990s.","Listen to Đurđevdan and then to Bregović's Ederlezi: they share a melody, and hearing both shows how Bregović returned to the same Romani tune in rock and film settings. Notice also how the album places folk and Romani music side by side with rock, reflecting the multi-ethnic idea of Yugoslavia that the record tried to celebrate.","The album was a big success, and Đurđevdan became one of the most recognisable songs in the region. Its message of unity has been read since as poignant and naive, a final statement of a shared Yugoslav identity before the war. Critics have also raised the question of credit for the traditional Romani material, a recurring issue in Bregović's career.","Start with Đurđevdan and hear how the Romani melody sits within rock production. Then listen to Bregović's Ederlezi for the film version of the same tune. Compare Sezen Aksu's Düğün ve Cenaze for a later collaboration across borders, and Fanfare Ciocărlia for the brass tradition behind much of this music."],"mercan-dede-su":["An album whose title means Water, in which Mercan Dede blends the sound of Sufi music with electronic beats and ambient textures. The breathy, haunting ney flute, the frame drum bendir and the plucked kanun weave over programmed rhythms and synthesiser pads, and guest singers bring folk and devotional melodies. The tracks flow into one another, building from meditative calm to hypnotic, dance-floor intensity.","Dede, born Arkın Ilıcalı in Turkey, moved to Canada and worked as a DJ in Montreal while studying the ney and Sufi traditions. He built a career split between Istanbul and North America, becoming one of the leading figures of world-music electronica in the early 2000s. His concerts often featured whirling dervishes alongside DJ sets, a provocative mix of the sacred and the club.","Listen to how the ney's breath sound is treated. Rather than cleaning it up, Dede often lets the breath and air noise come forward in the mix, making the flute's human texture part of the electronic soundscape. Notice too how the rhythms often follow Sufi ceremonial metres rather than club patterns, gradually accelerating as in a Mevlevi ritual.","The album was praised internationally for its seamless blending of traditions and became one of Dede's best-known records. Some traditionalists disapproved of placing sacred music in club settings, while others welcomed it as a way to bring young listeners to Sufi sounds. Critics generally rank it among the most successful marriages of electronic music and a living tradition.","Listen with good headphones and follow the ney's breath through the electronic layers. Notice where the beat first enters. Compare Dede Efendi's Mevlevi Âyin for the sacred music Dede draws on, and Brian Eno's ambient albums for the electronic atmosphere he works within."],"mercan-dede-800":["An album named for the 800th anniversary of the birth of Rumi, the thirteenth-century mystic poet whose followers founded the Mevlevi order. Dede again blends ney, frame drums and Turkish strings with electronic beats, but the mood is more devotional and the textures more orchestral. Long tracks build from quiet ney meditations into pulsing grooves, with chanted voices and swelling strings at their peaks.","2007 marked Rumi's 800th birth anniversary, celebrated internationally, with UNESCO declaring a year in his honour. Dede, whose musical identity was built on the Mevlevi tradition, made the album as a tribute. By then he was a familiar figure at international festivals, and his fusion style had influenced a wave of Turkish and Middle Eastern electronic musicians.","Listen for references to the structure of the Mevlevi ceremony, which moves through distinct sections with a gradual increase in intensity. Dede mirrors this arc across the album, so that the whole record works like a modern version of the whirling ritual. The ney, the central instrument of the Mevlevi rite, is always the first or last voice heard.","The album was well received, especially for its ambition in shaping a whole record around Rumi and the ceremony. Some critics felt the formula had become predictable compared with Su, while others found it his most spiritually coherent work. It remains a reference point in the genre of Sufi-inspired electronica.","Listen from start to finish to hear the ritual arc. Focus on the opening ney and then notice the first beat. Compare Dede Efendi's Ferahfeza Âyin for an original Mevlevi ceremony and Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan for another Sufi tradition pushed into new forms."],"altin-gun-gece":["The second album by the Amsterdam band Altın Gün, its title meaning Night. It consists of Turkish folk songs and classics of Anatolian rock reimagined with a modern psychedelic sound: electric saz, wah guitar, vintage keyboards and a tight, groovy rhythm section. The vocals alternate between Merve Daşdemir and Erdinç Ecevit, and the songs combine dreamy atmosphere with dance-floor energy.","Altın Gün was formed by Dutch bassist Jasper Verhulst, a fan of Turkish psychedelic records who sought out Turkish musicians in the Netherlands to form a band. Their sound drew on the Anatolian rock revival of the 2000s and 2010s, when Western labels reissued Turkish records from the 1970s. Gece was nominated for a Grammy for Best World Music Album, raising their international profile.","Listen to how the band adapts traditional melodies to modern grooves without losing their original modal character. Some songs are arranged from folk material that Erkin Koray, Selda Bağcan or Barış Manço had recorded decades earlier, so the album is partly a tribute and partly a reinterpretation. The electric saz gives a continuity with those older records.","The album was praised for its warmth and energy, and its Grammy nomination brought new attention to Anatolian rock. Some critics questioned whether a mostly Dutch band could represent Turkish music authentically, but the band's Turkish members and their care with the material won over most skeptics. It is now seen as a key album of the Anatolian rock revival.","Listen first for the electric saz and the interplay between the two singers. Then compare Erkin Koray's Elektronik Türküler or Selda Bağcan's 1976 album to hear the originals, and Tinariwen's Amassakoul for another band turning a regional tradition into hypnotic guitar music."],"altin-gun-yol":["Altın Gün's third album, its title meaning Road, shifts their sound toward synth-pop and electronic textures while keeping Turkish folk songs at the core. Drum machines, vintage synthesisers and dreamy production replace some of the earlier rock drive, and the electric saz takes a smaller role. The album feels lighter and more hypnotic, with repetitive grooves and soft, layered vocals.","Made largely during the COVID-19 pandemic, when the band could not tour or record together in the usual way, Yol was assembled through home recording and remote collaboration. The band drew on Turkish synth-pop and disco of the 1980s as well as folk and Anatolian rock. It was released to wide acclaim and further established them as a major name in global psychedelic music.","Listen for the way the drum machines echo the rhythms of Turkish folk dances in electronic form. Many tracks use repetitive patterns that recall both 1980s synth-pop and the trance-like repetition of folk music. The production also hides traditional instruments deep in the mix, so you may hear a saz or bağlama only on close listening.","The album was praised for its bold change of direction, with critics comparing it to classic synth-pop while noting its Turkish roots. Some fans missed the rock energy of Gece, but most saw Yol as a creative evolution. It cemented the band's reputation as more than revivalists, capable of reinventing their source material.","Listen with headphones to catch the hidden saz and the layered vocals. Notice how the synthesisers replace guitar riffs. Compare Gece for the band's rock sound, Selda Bağcan for the original folk-protest energy that some songs recall, and Kraftwerk's Radio-Activity for the drum-machine pulse behind it."],"ades-the-tempest":["A three-act opera on Shakespeare's play, sung to a rhymed libretto by Meredith Oakes that compresses the verse into short couplets. Prospero's anger drives the first act in thick, clanging brass; Caliban gets the warmest lyrical music; the lovers Miranda and Ferdinand sing in plain, rising lines. The sound everyone remembers is Ariel, a coloratura soprano kept almost permanently in the stratosphere, flickering above the orchestra like a spirit who cannot come down to human pitch.","The Royal Opera House commissioned it, and Adès conducted the first performance at Covent Garden in February 2004, with Simon Keenlyside as Prospero. He was thirty-two, already notorious for the chamber opera Powder Her Face, and this was his first full-scale work for a major house. Shakespeare's play had tempted and defeated many composers before him; Adès chose to have the text rewritten in short rhymed lines rather than set the original verse.","Adès builds much of the score on chains of intervals that widen or narrow step by step, so harmonies seem to drift between tonal gravity and weightlessness. Listen for how Prospero's music changes once he chooses forgiveness: the clangour thins, and Adès saves some of his plainest, most consonant writing for that moment. Ariel's first entry sits higher than almost any operatic role written before it.","The premiere was a triumph and the opera quickly travelled to Copenhagen, Strasbourg, Santa Fe and, in Robert Lepage's 2012 production, the Metropolitan Opera. Some critics regretted the loss of Shakespeare's language in Oakes's doggerel-like couplets; others argued it freed the music to carry the poetry. It is now one of the most performed British operas of its century, and the standard by which Adès's later stage works are measured.","Begin with the final scene of act three, where Caliban is left alone on the island singing a tender farewell that the orchestra dissolves around him. Then go back to Ariel's first appearance to hear how extreme the writing is. The Metropolitan Opera video with Adès conducting is the clearest introduction; compare Britten's Turn of the Screw for another English opera haunted by spirits."],"ades-violin-concerto-concentric-paths":["A twenty-minute concerto in three movements titled Rings, Paths and Rounds. The first is a fast, glittering spin in which the violin circles at high speed above flickering strings and wind. The long central movement, Paths, is the heart: a slow, grave procession in which orchestral lines move at different speeds and keep colliding with the soloist. The finale is a brisk, almost dance-like round that clears the air with surprising lightness.","Adès wrote it for the violinist Anthony Marwood, a longtime collaborator, and it was first played at the Berlin Festival in September 2005 with the Chamber Orchestra of Europe, conducted by the composer. It came in the year after The Tempest, when Adès was turning from large stage forms back toward compact abstract structures, and it rapidly became one of the most programmed new concertos of the decade.","The title is literal. Throughout Paths, groups of instruments trace cycles of different lengths, like planets on orbits of different sizes, so that the same material recurs out of phase and alignments happen only occasionally. The climax of the movement is one of those rare alignments, when the cycles coincide and the orchestra suddenly speaks with one voice. The solo part is less a melody than a route through these circles.","Critics welcomed it as one of the most substantial violin concertos by a British composer in decades, and it was taken up by orchestras across Europe and America within a few seasons. Some listeners find the outer movements slight beside the central one; most regard that imbalance as the design, the brief frames throwing the slow movement into relief. Other violinists have since taken it into their repertoire beyond Marwood.","Go straight to Paths and listen to how the slow orchestral layers seem to walk past each other before locking together. Then hear the first movement as a whirling prelude to it. Marwood's recording with Adès is the reference. Compare Ligeti's Violin Concerto for another late-twentieth-century concerto that treats the orchestra as a machine of interlocking cycles."],"ades-tevot":["A single span of about twenty-two minutes for a very large orchestra. It begins in the highest register, with violins and piccolos swarming in tiny overlapping figures, and slowly descends through the orchestra as if a vast vessel were settling into water. Waves of sound rise and break; brass chorales surface and sink; the ending is a long, warm, tonal glow that feels like reaching land after a voyage.","The Berlin Philharmonic and Simon Rattle commissioned it, and they gave the premiere in Berlin in February 2007 before taking it on tour. Adès was by then a regular partner of Rattle, who had championed Asyla in Birmingham a decade earlier. The title is a Hebrew word that can mean both the bars of music and an ark, including Noah's ark, and Adès described the orchestra as a ship carrying its world.","Almost everything grows from cascading lines, scales that fall in many parts at slightly different speeds, so the orchestra is constantly moving downward while seeming to stand still: an acoustic illusion of endless descent. Listen near the end for a broad melody in the brass that sounds like a quoted hymn but is entirely Adès's own, built from the same falling patterns slowed almost to stillness.","The premiere was warmly received, though some critics found the work's lush ending close to film music and questioned its earnestness after the irony of Asyla. Others heard it as Adès's most generous orchestral statement. It has entered the repertoire of orchestras on both sides of the Atlantic, and Rattle's recording with Berlin is often cited as one of the finest documents of their partnership.","Listen to the first two minutes twice: once for the dizzying high texture and once to notice how it slowly sinks. Then let the last five minutes play without interruption; the arrival of the final warm sonority is the point of the whole piece. Rattle's Berlin recording is the obvious choice. Compare Sibelius's Tapiola for another single-movement orchestral voyage."],"ades-the-exterminating-angel":["A three-act opera after Luis Buñuel's 1962 film, in which guests at a grand dinner party find they cannot leave the drawing room, for no reason anyone can name. Over days they starve, quarrel, fall ill and turn savage. Adès writes for a huge ensemble cast, each singer with a distinct vocal type, and threads the score with the eerie gliding tone of the ondes martenot, an early electronic instrument that seems to voice the invisible barrier itself.","The Salzburg Festival gave the premiere in July 2016, co-commissioned with the Royal Opera House and the Metropolitan Opera, where it arrived in 2017. Tom Cairns wrote the libretto with Adès and directed. Adès had wanted to set the film since his student years; its blend of surrealism, social satire and claustrophobia matched the darker, more ritualistic turn his music had taken since The Tempest.","The opera singer Leticia, one of the guests, is written so high that at the Met her opening phrase was reported as the highest note ever sung on that stage. The trap is musical as well as dramatic: whole passages recur with small variations, mirroring the repeated shots in Buñuel's film. Listen for distorted Spanish dances and tolling bells, and for the way the guests finally escape by re-enacting a moment from the start.","Reviews were mixed to enthusiastic. Many praised the orchestral writing and the ensemble scenes as Adès's most ambitious music; some found the opera long and the characters hard to distinguish, a problem inherited from the film's crowd of guests. Its staging by three major houses within eighteen months made it one of the most visible new operas of the decade, and debate continues over whether its coldness is a flaw or the point.","Start with the opening of act one, where guests arrive twice and the orchestra hints that something is wrong. Then jump to the final act's release and its bleak coda. The Royal Opera recording conducted by Adès is the best guide. Watch Buñuel's film first if you can, and compare Sciarrino's Luci mie traditrici for another opera of aristocratic claustrophobia."],"unsuk-chin-piano-etudes":["A set of short, ferociously difficult studies for solo piano, each built on one idea pushed to its limit. In C hammers a single pitch area until it splinters into resonance; Sequenzen sends patterns racing up and down at shifting speeds; Scherzo ad libitum lets the pianist reshuffle fragments; Toccata is a relentless machine of repeated notes; Grains breaks the piano's sound into tiny rattling particles. Together they last about twenty-five minutes and sound like a laboratory of piano colour.","Chin studied with György Ligeti in Hamburg in the 1980s, and Ligeti's own piano Études, begun in 1985, set the challenge she was answering. She wrote the first of hers in 1995 while living in Berlin, adding to the set over the following years. Young pianists took them up quickly, and they became a calling card for virtuosos interested in the repertoire that followed Ligeti and in music that treats the piano as a source of colour.","Chin's background in electronic music shows in the way the études treat the piano as a sound source to be processed. In several of them, the sustaining pedal is used to build up clouds of overtones that the hands then filter, so you hear notes the pianist never plays. Listen in Grains for the way fast repeated figures blur into an almost electronic granular texture.","The études were initially known only to specialists, but recordings and competition use have made them among the most performed new piano works of their generation. Some critics hear them as too dependent on Ligeti's model; others note that Chin's interest in timbre rather than rhythmic illusion gives them a separate identity. They are now regularly taught in conservatories as a bridge between Ligeti and later piano writing.","Start with the Toccata for sheer energy, then listen to Grains with headphones to catch the fizz of overtones. Play one of Ligeti's études alongside, perhaps L'escalier du diable, and notice how Chin cares less about rhythmic paradox and more about the colour of the sound. A recording that pairs Chin with Ligeti makes the ideal starting point."],"unsuk-chin-su":["A single-movement concerto for the sheng, the Chinese mouth organ of bamboo pipes, and orchestra. The sheng can sustain clusters of notes as long as the player breathes, inhaling or exhaling, so the piece opens in a shimmering haze in which the soloist and the strings are almost indistinguishable. Gradually the sheng asserts itself with sharp, reedy chords and fast pulsing figures, and the orchestra answers with glassy percussion and bright wind.","Chin wrote it for the sheng virtuoso Wu Wei, whose playing had transformed the instrument's profile in European new music. It was premiered in 2009 and quickly taken up by orchestras in Europe and Asia. Chin was then composer-in-residence with the Seoul Philharmonic and artistic director of its new-music series, and her interest in East Asian instruments within a Western orchestra was an explicit part of her programme.","The sheng was one of the instruments that, according to tradition, inspired the invention of the harmonium and accordion in Europe, so the concerto quietly closes a historical circle. Chin exploits the instrument's ability to sound while breathing in, so there are no breaks for air. Listen for passages where orchestral strings imitate the sheng's breathing cluster so closely that you lose track of where the soloist is.","Critics praised it as one of the few concertos for an Asian instrument that avoids decorative exoticism, treating the sheng as a modern instrument rather than a folk colour. Some found its middle stretches diffuse. Wu Wei's performances established it, and it is now one of the standard works in a growing repertoire for the sheng in symphonic settings.","Listen first for the opening haze and try to tell where the sheng ends and the orchestra begins. Then follow the soloist as it hardens into rhythmic chords. Wu Wei's recording with the Seoul Philharmonic is the obvious choice. Compare Chin's Violin Concerto for her treatment of a Western solo instrument, and Saariaho's Petals for another composer working with breath-like sustained sound."],"caroline-shaw-valencia":["A six-minute string quartet about an orange, specifically the Valencia variety. It begins with bright, pulsing chords, then breaks into scrubbing, glinting textures that suggest peeling, the spray of juice, the segments falling apart. Repeated rhythmic cells pass around the quartet like a game; by the end, the music has distilled to a gentle, sweet harmony that seems to hang in the air like a scent. It is direct, playful and surprisingly tender.","Shaw wrote it in 2012, the year she was finishing the Partita for 8 Voices that would win her the Pulitzer the next year. She plays violin and wrote it close to the instrument. The Attacca Quartet later made it part of their album Orange, devoted entirely to Shaw's quartets, which won the Grammy for Best Chamber Music Performance; the album title nods to this piece.","Shaw says the piece tries to capture the inside of an orange, its cells and structure, rather than its colour. Listen for the way the quartet splits into tiny repeated figures, like the vesicles inside each segment, that fit together into larger chords. The bowing techniques change constantly, pressure, position and speed, so the same harmony keeps changing colour.","It has become one of the most performed new string quartet pieces of its decade, programmed by student and professional quartets alike. Critics praise its economy and charm; a few find Shaw's quartets slight beside her larger works. Its success, with the rest of the Orange album, helped establish her as a chamber composer and not only a vocal one.","Put on the Attacca Quartet recording and simply follow the energy: the opening pulse, the splintering, the final calm. Then listen again for the different bowing sounds. Compare her Entr'acte, another quartet that uses old forms and textures in new ways, and Reich's Different Trains for another quartet built on pulse."],"caroline-shaw-narrow-sea":["A thirty-minute song cycle for soprano, piano and percussion quartet, built from texts in The Sacred Harp, the nineteenth-century American shape-note hymnal. Its sections drift through images of crossing water to a promised land. The percussion is gentle and resonant, metal and wood bowed, struck and rubbed, sometimes as soft as water. The voice moves between plain hymn-like melody and floating lines, while the piano provides quiet harmonic anchoring.","Shaw wrote it for the soprano Dawn Upshaw, the pianist Gilbert Kalish and Sō Percussion, who first performed it and recorded it for Nonesuch in 2021. It grew out of Shaw's long collaboration with Sō Percussion and her fascination with shape-note singing, a tradition of loud, open congregational hymnody still practiced in the American South and elsewhere.","The 'narrow sea' of the title comes from the hymn imagery of death as a short stretch of water between this life and the next. Listen for fragments that sound like actual shape-note tunes surfacing in the vocal line, transformed into quiet solos rather than the full-throated congregational sound of their origin, where dozens of singers face each other in a hollow square and sing at full volume.","Critics welcomed it as one of Shaw's most personal works and praised Upshaw's late-career performance, the recording drawing wide attention. Some listeners found its tranquillity monotonous over thirty minutes; others heard it as deliberately meditative. Its use of Sacred Harp material places it in a lineage running from Ives and Copland to more recent American explorations of vernacular sacred song.","Listen to it in one sitting with the lights low. Notice first the percussion colours, then the way the voice moves between hymn and murmur. If you can, hear an actual Sacred Harp singing afterwards to feel the contrast. The Nonesuch recording with Upshaw, Kalish and Sō Percussion is the definitive version. Compare Crumb's Ancient Voices of Children."],"julia-wolfe-lad":["Fourteen minutes for nine Highland bagpipes, often performed by a single piper playing live over a recording of the other eight. It starts with the drones settling in, then the chanters begin to overlap in tightly spaced, buzzing intervals, building a dense, roaring wall of sound that pulses and wavers. The effect is less a march than a slowly churning landscape, as raw and loud as any rock band.","Wolfe wrote it in 1995 for the piper Matt Welch, during the years when she, Michael Gordon and David Lang were running the Bang on a Can festival in New York and seeking a sound between minimalism and rock. Her interest in loud, physical music led her to the bagpipe, an instrument almost absent from concert music, with its fixed volume and unstoppable drone.","Because bagpipes cannot get softer or louder, Wolfe creates dynamics through density alone: the more pipes playing in close intervals, the more the sound seems to swell. Listen for the slow beating that arises when two chanters sit almost, but not quite, on the same pitch; it pulses like a heartbeat. The instrument's long association with military laments and funerals gives the roar an undertone of mourning.","The piece became something of a cult favourite, valued for its sheer acoustic force and as an emblem of Bang on a Can's commitment to unusual forces. Critics who found it one-dimensional were outweighed by those who heard in it a powerful combination of folk instrument and post-minimalist process. It is a regular programme choice when pipers explore contemporary repertoire.","Play it loud and let the wall of sound wash over you. Notice the slow beating between the pipes, almost like a heartbeat. Compare it with Wolfe's Cruel Sister for her use of a folk source, and with Reich's Drumming for another minimalist work built on accumulation. Seek a recording with a single piper over track for clarity."],"julia-wolfe-cruel-sister":["Half an hour for string orchestra, retelling an old British ballad in four connected sections. Two sisters walk by the sea; the elder pushes the younger in to drown out of jealousy; a minstrel makes a harp from the drowned girl's bones, and the harp sings out the murder. Wolfe never quotes the tune but builds the story from repeating rhythmic patterns, rising, swirling and churning, until the strings sound like waves, wind and finally the eerie voice of the bone-harp.","The Munich Chamber Orchestra commissioned it, and they premiered it in 2004. Wolfe had been exploring American and British folk sources and stories for years. The ballad, known as The Twa Sisters, survives in many versions throughout the Anglo-Celtic world, and Wolfe's version draws on the folk revival recordings of the 1960s as well as the old texts.","The ballad's refrain in many versions is a nonsense line repeated after each verse. Wolfe translates that recurrence into orchestral cells that return again and again, like the refrain heard across centuries. Listen for the section where the strings slide in glissandi as the younger sister drowns: the most graphic moment in the piece, and the turning point toward the bone-harp.","Reviewers praised its narrative energy and its demonstration that post-minimalist techniques could carry a story. Some found the length challenging. It has become one of Wolfe's most performed orchestral works and is often paired with her Fuel. Critics now see it as a key step toward the documentary oratorios, Steel Hammer and Anthracite Fields, that followed.","Read the ballad first, perhaps in a version sung by a folk revival singer, then follow the story through the music. Listen for the transformation from smooth waves to violent glissandi. The Ensemble Resonanz recording is excellent. Compare it with the traditional Twa Sisters to see how Wolfe turns a refrain into a structure."],"mazzoli-sinfonia-for-orbiting-spheres":["About eleven minutes of slowly turning orchestral sound. Strings, wind and percussion circle around a central harmonic pole, rising and falling in long arcs, while harmonicas played by orchestral musicians add a wheezing, breathy glow. The music feels like planets on elliptical orbits: no clear beginning or end, only waves that recur at different speeds and occasionally align in a bright, consonant swell.","Mazzoli first wrote it for chamber orchestra in 2014, then expanded it for full orchestra in 2016. Her music bridges the concert hall and the indie-rock scene of Brooklyn, where she also leads the band Victoire, and she was soon to become composer-in-residence with the Chicago Symphony Orchestra. The image of the solar system, she said, was a way to write a piece that never stops moving but never quite arrives.","Listen for the harmonicas, which most audiences do not notice until halfway through: they are handed to string or wind players and blown softly to create a reedy, slightly out-of-tune halo. The piece is a kind of passacaglia, built on a repeating chord pattern that keeps returning in new orchestral clothing, like a planet returning to the same place in its orbit.","Critics praised its warmth and orchestral imagination, and it has become one of Mazzoli's most performed orchestral works, played by major American orchestras. Some find its gentle surface too comfortable; others hear in its patient circles a rare confidence. It belongs to the run of work that led to Mazzoli's commission from the Metropolitan Opera.","Listen with headphones to catch the harmonica glow and the overlapping orbits. Notice how the piece seems to slow time down without ever stopping. Compare Ligeti's Lontano for another orchestral work of slowly shifting harmonies, and Adès's Tevot for an orchestral voyage. The Chicago Symphony performances are the reference."],"mazzoli-song-from-the-uproar":["A seventy-five-minute chamber opera about the Swiss explorer Isabelle Eberhardt, who dressed as a man, travelled across North Africa, converted to Islam and died in a flash flood at twenty-seven. The mezzo-soprano lead is surrounded by a vocal quintet acting as a chorus of inner voices, while a small ensemble of strings, piano, guitar, percussion and electronics creates layered, dreamlike textures. Projected films echo the desert landscapes and Eberhardt's journals.","Beth Morrison Projects produced the premiere in New York in 2012, part of a wave of new chamber operas emerging from New York's indie music scene. Mazzoli wrote the libretto with Royce Vavrek, who would later write her larger operas. She based it on Eberhardt's diaries and the ambiguities of her life, a woman crossing borders of gender, religion and nation decades before such stories were widely told.","The opera's title comes from Eberhardt's own journals, where she describes seeking a kind of music within the chaos of her life. Listen for the electric guitar and the softly droning electronics that mark the desert scenes, and for the way the chorus voices sometimes emerge from the ensemble itself. The final flood is written not as catastrophe but as a dissolving.","Critics praised its fusion of indie rock sound and operatic narrative, though some found the dramatic structure loose. It has been revived several times and recorded, and it marked Mazzoli as one of the leading voices of the new American chamber opera movement. Its success led directly to commissions for Breaking the Waves and the Metropolitan Opera.","Listen first to the opening, where the guitar and voices set a hazy, unmoored mood, then follow Eberhardt's journey to the final flood. The studio recording of the original production is the best guide. Compare it with Saariaho's L'Amour de loin, another opera about longing across distance, and with Reid's p r i s m for another new American chamber opera on a woman's inner life."],"thorvaldsdottir-metacosmos":["Thirteen minutes of vast, slowly shifting orchestral sound. It begins with low rumbling and the breath of brass instruments without pitch, then grows into huge sustained chords that swell and recede like a body breathing. Strings scrape and glide, percussion adds metallic shimmer, and at the climax the whole orchestra seems to be falling into a gravitational pull before the sound finally drains away into silence.","The New York Philharmonic commissioned it, and Esa-Pekka Salonen conducted the premiere in April 2018. Thorvaldsdóttir, who grew up in Iceland, had been gaining international attention since Aeriality. She described Metacosmos as inspired by the idea of falling into a black hole: the attraction, the fear and the beauty of an abyss, and the balance between chaos and order.","Thorvaldsdóttir composes from detailed hand-drawn sketches that look like landscapes, mapping density and texture before notes. Listen for the moment roughly two-thirds through when a soft, consonant chord emerges in the strings beneath the turmoil; it is the closest the piece gets to a melody, and it is quickly swallowed, as if by gravity.","Critics praised its scale and atmosphere, and it was quickly taken up by orchestras in Europe and America. Some find Thorvaldsdóttir's music static; supporters hear it as a deliberate rejection of conventional development in favour of a sense of geological time. Metacosmos is now one of the most frequently performed orchestral works by a living Nordic composer.","Listen in the dark and at a good volume, so the low rumbles and quiet breaths register. Notice how the orchestra behaves like a single organism. Compare her Aeriality for an earlier exploration of the same approach, and Ligeti's Atmosphères for the ancestor of this kind of orchestral cloud. The Iceland Symphony recording is the reference."],"thorvaldsdottir-catamorphosis":["Twenty minutes of slowly gathering orchestral weather. Low strings and brass build long, shifting chords; breathy noise from wind and percussion swirls around them; occasional shafts of light appear in the high strings, only to be overwhelmed. The music moves in vast arcs, growing denser and more unstable, until a final quiet passage hangs suspended, as if the world were waiting to see whether it would survive.","The Berlin Philharmonic co-commissioned it with other orchestras, and Kirill Petrenko conducted the premiere in Berlin in January 2021, in a hall emptied by pandemic restrictions and streamed online. Thorvaldsdóttir described it as being about the fragile relationship between humanity and the planet: the forces that hold the earth together and the ones that are pulling it apart. It came at a moment when concert life itself had nearly stopped.","The title combines Greek roots for 'down' and 'form' and suggests a transformation toward a lesser state, a decline. Listen for a rising figure in the strings that returns several times; each time it reaches a little less high, as if the energy for ascent were running out. The final pages are among the quietest she has written for orchestra.","The premiere drew wide attention despite the absence of an audience, and critics praised it as her most powerful orchestral statement. Some found its environmental programme too explicit; others felt it was deeply felt rather than didactic. Performances by other orchestras quickly followed, and it confirmed Thorvaldsdóttir's position as one of the leading orchestral composers of her generation.","Listen to it in one sitting and let the arcs build. Pay attention to the rising string figure and its gradual exhaustion. Compare Metacosmos for the same composer's vision of cosmic gravity, and Sibelius's Tapiola for an earlier Nordic orchestral work about elemental forces. The Iceland Symphony Orchestra's recording is an excellent guide to the textures."],"abrahamsen-let-me-tell-you":["A half-hour cycle for soprano and orchestra in three parts, in which Ophelia from Hamlet tells her own story. The orchestra is luminous and fragile: high strings, celesta, soft brass, and percussion brushed with paper and fingers. The voice moves between stammering, repeated syllables and long, floating lines. In the last part, Ophelia walks out into a snowy landscape, and the music dissolves into whispering white noise and tiny glints of sound.","Abrahamsen wrote it for the Canadian soprano Barbara Hannigan, who premiered it with the Berlin Philharmonic and Andris Nelsons in December 2013. The text is by the writer Paul Griffiths, from his 2008 novel of the same name, in which Ophelia is allowed to use only the words she speaks in Shakespeare's play. The piece won the Grawemeyer Award in 2016 and quickly became Abrahamsen's best-known work.","Because Griffiths restricted Ophelia's vocabulary to her lines in Hamlet, her sentences have an oddly stilted, reaching quality; Abrahamsen mirrors this by having her stutter and repeat words as if searching for meaning. Listen in the last section for the sound of snow: paper rustled, brushes on drums, high harmonics, the closest music gets to silence.","Critics greeted it with unusual unanimity, calling it one of the finest vocal works of its century. Hannigan's performances and her recording with the Bavarian Radio Symphony made it famous. Some find its fragility precious; most hear it as a perfect fusion of text, voice and orchestra. It is now one of the most performed new works for soprano and orchestra.","Read a few of Ophelia's lines from Hamlet first, then listen to the opening to hear how the singer stumbles over them. Let the final section play in silence. Hannigan's recording is essential. Compare Kurtág's Kafka Fragments for another fragmented vocal work, and Abrahamsen's Schnee for the snow imagery in purely instrumental form."],"abrahamsen-schnee":["Fifty-five minutes of ten canons for nine players, arranged in pairs, each pair exploring a different type of musical repetition. The sound is hushed and glacial: two pianos, strings in muted harmonics, wind instruments breathing softly, all moving in slow, calm patterns like snow drifting across a frozen landscape. The pairs grow shorter and more fragile as the work proceeds, until a final pair that seems almost to melt away into silence.","Abrahamsen wrote it after a long creative silence in the 1990s, during which he arranged other composers' music rather than writing his own. It premiered in Germany in 2008 and was soon recognised as a turning point in his career. The idea came from his fascination with canons and with the image of snow, which he associated with childhood in Denmark and the stillness of winter.","The canons are not strict imitations but explore different ways of repeating material: forwards, backwards, at different speeds, with different instruments. Listen for the moments between canons, when the players perform short intermezzos with paper rustling and soft bowing. One piano is tuned a quarter-tone lower than the other, giving the whole piece a subtle, uncanny glow.","Critics hailed it as one of the major chamber works of the century, and the Ensemble Recherche recording brought it to wide attention. Some find its length and stasis demanding; admirers hear in it a profound meditation on time and repetition. It established Abrahamsen as a major voice, paving the way for let me tell you.","Listen to the first pair of canons, then step away and return to another pair. The piece rewards patient, unhurried attention. Notice how the slightly mistuned pianos create a shimmer. Compare Feldman's Triadic Memories for another work of extended quiet repetition, and Abrahamsen's let me tell you for the snow imagery in vocal form."],"haas-morgen-und-abend":["A ninety-minute opera in two parts, on the birth and death of an ordinary Norwegian fisherman. The first part, morning, is the night of his birth, with his father waiting; the second, evening, is his last day, when he wanders through his village meeting the dead. Haas writes in microtonal harmonies drawn from the natural overtones of low notes, so the orchestra glows with strangely pure, beating chords that seem to hover between life and death.","The Royal Opera House and the Deutsche Oper Berlin co-commissioned it, and it was premiered in London in November 2015, then in Berlin. The libretto is by the Norwegian writer Jon Fosse, adapted from his own novel. Haas, an Austrian composer living in New York, had been exploring overtone harmony for decades, and here applied it to a gentle, contemplative narrative.","The opera opens with a long, dark passage in which the orchestra slowly builds an overtone chord in near darkness; the audience waits as the father waits. Listen in the second part for the moment when the fisherman realises he has died: the harmonies become purer and calmer, as if the beating of the microtonal chords were finally stilled.","Reviews were divided: some critics found it static and the drama undernourished, while others heard it as a meditative work of rare harmonic beauty and praised the restraint of the staging. Its presentation by two major opera houses gave spectral harmony an unusually prominent platform. It is now cited as a key example of overtone-based thinking applied to the stage, alongside Haas's other operas.","Listen to the opening long passage in a dark room and let the harmonies settle in. In the second part, pay attention to the quiet, gentle music of the fisherman's last walk. Compare Haas's in vain for his orchestral writing, and Grisey's Quatre chants pour franchir le seuil for another spectral meditation on death."],"rebecca-saunders-skin":["A thirty-minute work for soprano and thirteen instruments, built from fragments of text about skin as a boundary between self and world. The voice ranges from whispers and breathy murmurs to sudden, piercing high notes. The ensemble surrounds the singer with dense, prickly textures: scraped strings, muted brass, bowed percussion, sudden explosions of sound followed by long, held silences. It feels intimate, physical and raw.","Saunders wrote it for the soprano Juliet Fraser and Klangforum Wien, who premiered it in 2016. The text is her own, assembled from fragments with a strong debt to Samuel Beckett's late prose. Saunders, born in London and based in Berlin since her studies with Wolfgang Rihm, has long explored the physicality of sound, and Skin brought that approach to the human voice on a large scale.","The work's silences are as important as its sounds: Saunders marks precise durations of silence, and the musicians remain frozen, so tension builds even when nothing is heard. Listen for the moments when the soprano's breath becomes audible, and for the way instruments imitate the voice, blurring the line between human and mechanical sound.","Critics praised its intensity and Fraser's extraordinary performance. Some found its fragmentation hard to follow; others saw it as Saunders's most personal and accomplished work. It came shortly before she received the Ernst von Siemens Music Prize in 2019, one of the highest honours in contemporary music, which confirmed her standing as a leading European composer.","Listen with headphones and in a quiet room, so you can hear the whispered sounds and the silences. Focus first on the voice, then on how the instruments respond. Compare Berio's Sequenza III for another work that explores vocal extremes, and Kurtág's Kafka Fragments for fragmented texts. Seek the recording by Fraser and Klangforum Wien."],"rebecca-saunders-chroma":["A spatial composition for chamber groups placed throughout a building, often a gallery or atrium, with the audience free to wander. Each group plays fragments of sound that overlap in different combinations depending on where you stand: sustained notes, sudden bursts, scratchy textures, quiet clicks and rattles. The piece can last up to an hour and changes from version to version, so no two performances are alike.","Saunders first created Chroma in 2003 and has adapted it many times for different spaces, adjusting the number and placement of groups to the architecture. It grew out of her interest in sound as a physical, spatial material, and her collaborations with visual artists. The title refers to colour, and she treats sounds as pigments arranged in space.","Each version of Chroma is a collage drawn from a pool of material, so even the composer cannot predict exactly how a given listener will hear it. Listen for the quietest groups, often barely audible from a distance, which give the piece a fragile undertone amid the harsher textures. Moving through the space changes the balance radically, so your walk becomes part of the form.","Critics praised its originality and the way it transforms familiar spaces into acoustic environments. Some found it more installation than composition; supporters argued it challenges the idea of a fixed concert work. It has become one of Saunders's signature projects, performed in major venues across Europe.","If possible, experience it live and walk through the space, noticing how the sound changes as you move. With a recording, use headphones and imagine yourself in different positions. Compare Stockhausen's Gruppen for an earlier spatial work, and Nono's Prometeo for music that dissolves into space. Seek a live performance if you can."],"jessie-montgomery-strum":["A seven-minute piece for strings in which the players strum their instruments like guitars or banjos, creating bright, rhythmic textures. Over this pulsing foundation, short melodies rise and fall, influenced by American folk, dance and popular music. The piece builds to an exuberant climax, then fades into a gentle, shimmering close.","Montgomery wrote the first version in 2006 and revised it in 2012 for the Catalyst Quartet, with whom she played violin. Born in New York, she grew up on the Lower East Side in an artistic household, steeped in jazz, folk and experimental music. Strum reflects her wish to write vivid, accessible music that draws on all of those influences while remaining rooted in string playing.","The strumming is not just decorative; it creates a rhythmic web that interlocks between the players, like a folk band. Listen for the cello's role, which shifts from bass to melody. The piece's harmonies echo bluegrass and blues while remaining grounded in classical string techniques.","Strum has become one of Montgomery's most performed works, embraced by quartets and orchestras alike for its energy and charm. Critics praise its fresh sound and its joyful embrace of American vernacular traditions. It helped establish her as a leading voice in contemporary American music.","Listen for the strumming technique first, then notice how the melodies emerge over it. Compare it with Shaw's Valencia for another bright, rhythmic quartet, and Dvořák's American Quartet for an earlier evocation of American music by a visitor. The Catalyst Quartet's recording captures its energy best, though the string orchestra version gives the strumming more weight."],"jessie-montgomery-banner":["About ten minutes for string quartet and string orchestra, written as a meditation on national anthems. Fragments of The Star-Spangled Banner, Lift Every Voice and Sing and other anthems appear and are transformed, layered, fractured and woven into a complex tapestry. The music shifts between hymn-like passages, energetic rhythms and moments of tension and dissonance.","Montgomery wrote it in 2014 for the Sphinx Organization, which supports Black and Latino musicians, to mark the two hundredth anniversary of the writing of The Star-Spangled Banner. She wanted to explore what an anthem means in a diverse and divided country, asking whose story it tells and who it includes.","Listen for the moment when fragments of Lift Every Voice and Sing, often called the Black national anthem, emerge and intertwine with The Star-Spangled Banner. Montgomery also weaves in melodies from other countries' anthems, suggesting a broader, more inclusive vision of patriotism.","Banner has been widely performed and praised for its thoughtful exploration of national identity. Critics highlight its emotional power and its timely questions about inclusion and belonging, especially in seasons when orchestras sought new ways to open concerts. It has become a centrepiece of programmes celebrating American diversity and helped establish Montgomery as one of the most performed living American composers.","Listen for the anthem quotations and how they are transformed. Notice the interplay between the solo quartet and the larger string orchestra. Compare Ives's Variations on America for an earlier take on patriotic material, and Still's Afro-American Symphony for another work centring Black American experience."],"gabriela-lena-frank-leyendas":["An eighteen-minute work in six movements for string quartet, evoking the music and landscapes of the Andes. Each movement imitates a different traditional sound: panpipes, the charango, funeral songs, dances. The strings use extended techniques to mimic these instruments, creating vivid, colourful textures. The movements range from lively and rhythmic to mournful and reflective.","Frank, born in California to a Peruvian mother of partly Chinese descent and a Lithuanian-Jewish father, wrote Leyendas in 2001, inspired by her travels in Peru. She wanted to explore her heritage and the idea of mestizaje, the blending of cultures. The piece has become one of her most popular works, performed by quartets worldwide.","Listen in the movement titled Himno de Zampoñas for the way the strings imitate panpipes, with players alternating notes to create the characteristic hocketing effect. The movement Chasqui depicts a runner carrying messages across the Inca empire, with fast, driving rhythms.","Leyendas has been praised for its vivid imagery and its fusion of Andean folk elements with classical string writing. It has become a staple of the string quartet repertoire, particularly among younger ensembles. Some critics see it as a model of how to integrate folk traditions authentically.","Listen to each movement as a separate scene, noticing how the strings mimic different instruments. Start with Himno de Zampoñas for its panpipe effects, then Chasqui for its speed. Compare Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances for another composer integrating folk music, and Ginastera's Estancia for South American influences. Both the quartet and string orchestra versions are worth hearing."],"gabriela-lena-frank-el-ultimo-sueno":["A two-act opera in Spanish about the painters Frida Kahlo and Diego Rivera, set on the Day of the Dead. Diego, dying, calls Frida back from the land of the dead, and she returns for one day to revisit their turbulent love. The music blends Mexican folk idioms, mariachi colours and lush orchestral writing, with vivid vocal lines and colourful choral scenes.","San Diego Opera premiered it in October 2022, co-commissioned with other houses. The libretto is by the Pulitzer Prize-winning playwright Nilo Cruz. Frank had long wanted to write an opera, and the story of Frida and Diego allowed her to explore themes of love, art, death and Latin American identity.","Listen for the way Frank threads Mexican popular sounds, guitars, dance rhythms, the brightness of mariachi brass, through an orchestra that otherwise speaks the language of the modern opera house. The chorus of the dead, singing in folk-like harmonies, frames the action and returns at the close, so that Frida's single day among the living feels enclosed by the world she must go back to.","Critics praised its vibrant score and its celebration of Latin American culture, and it has been performed at several major American houses. Some found the drama uneven; others hailed it as a landmark in American opera for its representation of Latin American stories.","Listen for the Mexican folk elements woven into the orchestral fabric, and the vivid characterisations of Frida and Diego. Compare it with Ginastera's Estancia for South American influences, and with Mazzoli's Song from the Uproar for another opera about a woman artist's life."],"anna-clyne-this-midnight-hour":["A twelve-minute orchestral work inspired by two poems, one by Juan Ramón Jiménez and one by Charles Baudelaire. It opens with a fierce, driving rhythm in the low strings, then breaks into a series of contrasting episodes: a mysterious waltz, a lyrical melody, swirling textures. The music has a cinematic quality, full of energy and dramatic shifts.","Clyne wrote it in 2015 for the Orchestre national d'Île-de-France, where she held a residency, after five years as a composer-in-residence with the Chicago Symphony Orchestra. Born in London and long based in the United States, she had become one of the most performed orchestral composers of her generation. The piece reflects her love of narrative and imagery, drawing on poetry to create vivid orchestral scenes.","Listen for the waltz section, which seems to emerge from nowhere, like a ghostly dance at midnight, and then slips away before it can settle. The fierce opening rhythm returns in transformed guises throughout the piece, sometimes as a whisper in the strings, sometimes as a full orchestral assault, unifying what might otherwise feel like a sequence of unrelated episodes.","This Midnight Hour has become one of Clyne's most performed orchestral works, played by orchestras in Europe and America and often used as a concert opener. Critics praise its energy, its vivid orchestration and its sense of theatre. A few find its cinematic surface too easy; most hear it as a confident example of new orchestral music that audiences grasp at first hearing.","Listen for the driving opening rhythm in the low strings, then follow the piece through its contrasting episodes and notice when that rhythm comes back. Compare it with Ravel's La Valse for another ghostly waltz that unravels, and with Clyne's own Night Ferry for another orchestral journey drawn from literature. Any committed modern orchestral recording will serve."],"anna-clyne-within-her-arms":["A fourteen-minute elegy for fifteen solo strings, written in memory of the composer's mother. The music unfolds slowly, with long, overlapping lines that create a rich, warm texture, each player holding an individual part rather than doubling. It builds gradually to an intense climax, then fades into a quiet, consoling close. The sound is intimate, unhurried and deeply emotional from the first chord.","Clyne wrote it in 2009, shortly after the death of her mother, while she was establishing herself in New York. It was one of the pieces that first brought her wide attention in the United States. The work reflects her personal grief and her wish to create a musical tribute that would capture the warmth and protection she associated with her mother.","The piece is built on a simple, descending melodic idea that is passed between the instruments, creating a sense of embrace. Listen for the moment near the end when the texture thins to a single line, like a final farewell.","Within Her Arms has been widely performed and praised for its emotional directness and beautiful string writing. Critics see it as one of Clyne's most personal works, and it has become a favourite among string ensembles and chamber orchestras. Some find it close to familiar models of string elegy; admirers hear in that closeness a deliberate continuity with a tradition of mourning music.","Listen to it in a quiet moment, letting the long lines unfold and overlap. Notice how the fifteen individual parts make the texture seem to breathe. Compare it with Barber's Adagio for Strings for another famous string elegy, and with Pärt's Cantus in Memory of Benjamin Britten for another meditation on loss built from slowly descending lines."],"tyshawn-sorey-monochromatic-light-afterlife":["A work of about seventy-five minutes for bass-baritone, viola, piano and celesta, percussion and chorus, written for the Rothko Chapel in Houston. The music is slow, spacious and contemplative, with long silences and sustained tones that hang in the air. The voice sings fragments of spirituals and wordless lines, while the instruments create a hushed, meditative atmosphere that seems to match the dark, almost monochrome paintings.","Sorey wrote it to mark the fiftieth anniversary of the Rothko Chapel, where Morton Feldman's Rothko Chapel had been premiered in 1972. It was first heard at the chapel in 2022, then staged by Peter Sellars at the Park Avenue Armory in New York. Sorey, a drummer, composer and multi-instrumentalist, has long explored the boundaries between jazz, improvisation and composed music.","The work is a direct response to Feldman's Rothko Chapel, sharing much of its instrumentation, viola, celesta, percussion and chorus. Listen for the moments when the bass-baritone sings fragments of spirituals, linking the chapel's abstraction to African American musical traditions that Feldman's piece did not touch, and for the long silences that Sorey treats as part of the music.","Critics praised it as a profound meditation on spirituality, memory and race, and the New York staging was widely acclaimed. Some found its extreme slowness challenging over more than an hour; others saw it as the most significant response yet to Feldman's work. It consolidated Sorey's reputation as a composer of concert music alongside his career as a drummer and improviser.","Listen in a quiet, contemplative setting, letting the long silences and sustained sounds wash over you. Do not wait for events; let the colours change slowly. Then listen to Feldman's Rothko Chapel to hear what Sorey inherits and what he adds. If possible, look at images of the chapel's paintings while listening."],"tyshawn-sorey-adagio-for-wadada-leo-smith":["A concerto for alto saxophone and orchestra of roughly half an hour, slow throughout, as the title promises. The saxophone plays long, sustained lines, often quiet and introspective, while the orchestra provides a spacious, slowly shifting backdrop of soft chords and isolated colours. There are moments of intense lyricism and passages close to silence, and the soloist seems to be thinking aloud rather than displaying technique.","Sorey wrote it for the saxophonist Steven Banks, who gave the premiere in 2023. It is dedicated to the trumpeter and composer Wadada Leo Smith, a key figure of the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians and a mentor to Sorey. The concerto won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in 2024, a year after Sorey had been a finalist.","Sorey's background in improvised music shapes the way the solo line is paced: phrases often feel as though they are being discovered in real time, and the orchestra waits for them. Listen for the moments when soloist and orchestra seem to breathe together, and for the way silence is held long enough to become part of the melody, a lesson Sorey credits partly to Smith.","The Pulitzer brought it wide attention, and critics praised its depth and its fusion of an improviser's sensibility with orchestral writing. Some found its unrelieved slowness demanding in a concerto, a form that usually promises display; others saw that refusal as the point, and as a bold statement about what a soloist can be.","Listen with patience, letting the long lines unfold, and focus on the saxophone's tone and phrasing rather than on what happens next. Compare it with Feldman's late works for another exploration of slow time, and with Anthony Braxton's For Alto for the saxophone's place in experimental Black music. Banks's performances are the reference."],"turnage-greek":["An eighty-minute opera in two acts, based on Steven Berkoff's play, which updates the Oedipus myth to London's East End in the 1980s. Eddy, the hero, leaves home, kills a man in a café brawl, and marries his widow, only to discover she is his mother. The music is raw and aggressive, with jazz and rock influences, brass and percussion prominent.","Turnage wrote it for the first Munich Biennale, where it was premiered in 1988, at the invitation of Hans Werner Henze, the festival's director and his mentor. He was then a young composer steeped in jazz and soul as well as Stravinsky. Greek captured the anger and violence of Thatcher-era Britain, its riots and strikes, and became a defining work of its generation.","Listen for the café fight scene, where the music becomes percussive and violent and the singers are pushed close to shouting. The opera ends with Eddy refusing to blind himself: he rejects the tragic fate of Oedipus and insists on his love, a defiant twist taken from Berkoff that turns the myth's fatalism inside out.","Greek was a sensation at its premiere and at the Edinburgh Festival soon after, and it has been widely staged and filmed. Critics praised its energy, its foul-mouthed vigour and its fusion of classical and popular styles; some found it crude. It made Turnage's reputation and remains one of the most performed British operas of its decade.","Listen for the jazz and rock influences in the score, and the raw energy of the vocal writing. Compare it with Weill's Threepenny Opera for another opera of urban grit, and with Birtwistle's Punch and Judy for another violent British opera."],"turnage-blood-on-the-floor":["A seventy-minute suite in nine movements for large ensemble with jazz soloists on guitar, saxophone and drums. The movements range from fierce, driving numbers to slow, mournful elegies. The jazz soloists improvise within the composed structure, creating a fusion of classical and jazz idioms. The title comes from a painting by Francis Bacon.","Turnage wrote it between 1993 and 1996 for Ensemble Modern, with jazz musicians including the guitarist John Scofield and the drummer Peter Erskine. The work reflects his deep love of jazz and his desire to bridge the gap between concert music and improvisation. One movement, Elegy for Andy, mourns his brother, who died of a drug overdose.","Listen for Elegy for Andy, a slow, heartfelt movement that is the emotional centre of the work, written for the composer's brother. The interplay between the composed ensemble parts and the improvised solos creates constant tension and release: the written music often seems to stop and listen to the soloist, then absorbs what it has heard into the next section.","Blood on the Floor was widely acclaimed as a landmark in the meeting of jazz and concert music, more convincing to many than earlier 'third stream' attempts because the improvisers are genuinely free. Critics praised its energy and emotional depth. It has been recorded and revived, and it opened the way for Turnage's many later collaborations with jazz musicians.","Start with the Elegy for Andy, then explore the more energetic movements. Listen for the jazz soloists and how they interact with the ensemble. Compare it with Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige for another large-scale jazz composition, and Miles Davis's Bitches Brew for jazz-rock fusion."],"ellen-reid-prism":["An opera of about seventy-five minutes about a mother and daughter living in isolation after a traumatic event. The music is dreamlike and shimmering, built from a small ensemble, electronics and a chorus, with sounds that seem to come from inside the daughter's head. The opera moves between a sealed, fairy-tale world and painful reality, as the daughter slowly confronts what happened to her and what her mother has hidden.","Reid wrote it with the librettist Roxie Perkins, and it was premiered by LA Opera and Beth Morrison Projects in Los Angeles in November 2018, later travelling to New York's Prototype Festival. The opera addresses sexual assault and its aftermath, arriving at the height of public reckoning over abuse. It won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in 2019, bringing Reid sudden national attention.","The opera's sound world changes as the daughter's understanding changes: the opening is all shimmer and sweetness, while later scenes let harsher, more distorted sounds break through. Listen for how the chorus and electronics blur the boundary between what is real and what is remembered, so that the structure itself mirrors the fragmented memory of trauma.","Critics praised its sensitivity and its inventive use of sound. The Pulitzer brought it wide attention and revivals. Some found its abstraction and dream logic challenging; others saw it as a powerful and responsible treatment of a subject opera has often exploited, and as evidence of how much chamber opera had changed in a decade.","Listen with headphones to appreciate the layering of voices and electronics, and notice how the sweetness of the opening gradually curdles. Compare it with Mazzoli's Song from the Uproar for another new American chamber opera produced by the same company, and with Saariaho's L'Amour de loin for similarly luminous, shimmering textures."],"preisner-the-double-life-of-veronique":["Music for Krzysztof Kieślowski's film about two identical young women, one in Kraków and one in Clermont-Ferrand, who never meet but seem to share one life. At its centre is a grave, slowly climbing concert piece for soprano, chorus and orchestra, its line rising by small, aching steps over a bed of sustained strings. Around it the score is sparse: a guitar, a recorder melody, a few bars of piano, and silence. The same tune keeps returning in different clothes, which is the film's whole idea.","Preisner and Kieślowski had already worked together on Decalogue, the ten-part television cycle of 1988, and this was their first international co-production, made with French money in the last months of Communist Poland. Kieślowski wanted music that the characters themselves could hear and perform, so Preisner wrote a score that exists inside the story: a choir rehearsal, a concert, a schoolroom of children playing recorders. The film premiered at Cannes in 1991 and made both men famous outside Poland.","The concert piece is credited within the film to Van den Budenmayer, an invented Dutch composer Kieślowski and Preisner created as a private joke and an alibi for writing grand, old-fashioned music. He returns in Decalogue and in the Three Colours trilogy, giving the films a shared imaginary past. Listen for the children's recorder lesson in the French half: it plays the very melody that the Polish Weronika was singing when her heart failed.","Critics in 1991 were divided on the film, some finding it too mysterious to bear its own weight, but nearly all singled out the music. The score is now often cited as one of the purest examples of music as plot rather than accompaniment, since a melody is the thread connecting two strangers. Some find the soprano writing overripe; admirers answer that the excess belongs to the invented composer, not to Preisner.","Hear the full concert cue first, then go back to the shorter fragments and notice how each is a piece of it, broken off and carried somewhere else. Watching the film's concert scene once makes the climb of the soprano line unforgettable. Then turn to Preisner's score for Three Colours: Blue, where the same invented composer resurfaces in a much darker context."],"preisner-three-colours-blue":["The score for the first film of Kieślowski's Three Colours trilogy, about a woman named Julie who loses her husband, a celebrated composer, and their daughter in a car crash. Its centrepiece is the Song for the Unification of Europe, a vast choral and orchestral anthem her husband left unfinished, sung in Greek. Against that public grandeur sit tiny private fragments: a funeral march, a piano phrase picked out by one finger, a recorder played by a busker in the street outside.","The trilogy took its colours from the French flag and its themes from the revolutionary motto, with Blue standing for liberty. It was made in 1992 and 1993, as the Maastricht Treaty was being argued over across the continent, so an unfinished hymn for a newly unified Europe carried a real public charge. The film won the Golden Lion at Venice in 1993, and its music, which the heroine may well have secretly written, is its central mystery.","The text of the Song for the Unification of Europe is Saint Paul's hymn to love from the First Letter to the Corinthians, sung in the original Greek, and it only emerges once Julie stops running from her grief. Listen too for the musical blackouts: several times the score surges in, the screen fades to black, and then the scene simply resumes, as though the music had swallowed a moment of time.","The score became Preisner's best-known work and sold widely on CD in the mid-1990s, when European art-house film music briefly found a mass audience. Some critics found the Unification anthem bombastic, which is arguably the point: it is the official, public music that Julie has to reshape into something private. Debate continues over whether the film implies that she was the real author of her husband's work.","Start with the full Song for the Unification of Europe, then listen to the short piano statements of its melody to hear what the film is really about: a huge public statement whittled down to one person's hand at a keyboard. Watch the film's sugar-cube scene for how the music and silence trade places. Compare with Preisner's later Requiem for My Friend, written in Kieślowski's memory."],"delerue-black-robe":["One of the last scores Georges Delerue completed, for Bruce Beresford's film about a young French Jesuit travelling up the frozen rivers of New France in the 1630s with Algonquin guides. The music is spacious and grave: long, singing string lines, a solemn chorus that feels like a liturgy heard across water, and horn calls that sound against wide silences. Delerue avoids any pastiche of Indigenous music, writing instead from inside the priest's European faith and its slow unravelling.","Delerue, French by birth, had been living and working in Los Angeles since the early 1980s, where he scored dozens of Hollywood dramas after his celebrated years with the French New Wave. Black Robe was a Canadian and Australian co-production adapted from Brian Moore's novel, and it gave Delerue the kind of serious, landscape-scale drama he rarely got from Hollywood in those years. He died in Los Angeles in March 1992, shortly after its release.","Listen for how rarely the chorus sings words: it is used as a colour, the sound of a cathedral imagined in a forest, so that the missionary's faith is present as an atmosphere rather than a statement. The recurring theme is also built in long arches with almost no rhythmic push, which makes the music feel like the canoe journey itself, steady, cold and without a visible destination.","Black Robe was admired on release for refusing both romance and condescension in its portrait of seventeenth-century contact between missionaries and Indigenous nations, though some critics argued it still centred the European viewpoint. The score has long been treasured by Delerue's admirers as a late summation of his gift for elegy, and it is often named alongside his very different work for Truffaut and Godard as the best of his career.","Begin with the main title, which states the long string theme in full, then listen to a choral cue and notice how it never resolves into a hymn tune. Compare it with Delerue's theme for Godard's Le Mépris from 1963 to hear the same melancholy in a far smaller frame. For a kindred landscape score, put it beside Morricone's The Mission."],"tiersen-amelie":["The soundtrack to Jean-Pierre Jeunet's Le Fabuleux Destin d'Amélie Poulain, a fairy tale about a shy Montmartre waitress who secretly rearranges other people's lives. Most of the record is small instruments played by one person: an accordion waltz, a toy piano tinkling over a bass line, a harpsichord, a mandolin, a melodica, a typewriter-like rhythm. The piano piece known as Comptine d'un autre été, a rippling left hand under a simple repeating tune, became the record's calling card.","Tiersen, a Breton multi-instrumentalist with an indie rock background, had released several albums of short, homemade instrumentals by the late 1990s. Jeunet chose to build much of the film's music from those existing records, adding new pieces, so the soundtrack is partly a compilation of Tiersen's earlier work. Released in 2001, the film was an enormous international hit and the album became one of the best-selling French soundtracks of its era.","Because so much of the music predates the film, some of its best-known pieces were never written for Amélie at all and carry titles from their original albums. Listen to how often the waltzes are slightly lopsided, the accordion and toy piano not quite in step, which is what keeps the sweetness from cloying. The arrangements are thin enough that you can count every instrument.","The soundtrack was a commercial phenomenon and its accordion waltz became a shorthand for a picture-postcard Paris, which some French critics resented as nostalgic and sanitised, the same complaint made against the film. Tiersen himself has distanced himself from the Amélie sound, moving towards electronics and the landscape of Ushant, the island where he lives. Its piano pieces remain fixtures of amateur piano playing worldwide.","Start with the accordion waltz, then the solo piano Comptine, and listen for how much of the effect comes from repetition with tiny changes. Then hear one of Tiersen's own albums, such as Le Phare, to understand where the music came from. Put Satie's Gymnopédies beside it: the same plain, slightly odd French tenderness, a century earlier."],"tiersen-good-bye-lenin":["Tiersen's score for Wolfgang Becker's tragicomedy about an East Berlin son who hides the fall of the Wall from his fragile, devoutly socialist mother. The music is mostly piano and strings: plain, circling figures that gather into rising, nostalgic swells, with a few accordion and song cues for colour. Where Amélie twinkled, this score aches; its main piano theme moves slowly and repeats with an almost childlike patience.","Becker's film, released in 2003, was one of the first great German popular successes about reunification, and the music had to hold both comedy and mourning for a lost country. Tiersen, newly famous after Amélie, wrote mostly new material, recorded with a small string section. The film's subject, the strange nostalgia that came to be called Ostalgie, gave his wistful style a much heavier historical charge than in Paris.","The son invents fake news broadcasts to preserve his mother's vanished East Germany, and the score quietly mirrors this: its cues are music about a world that is gone but kept alive by repetition. Listen to how the piano figures loop without arriving anywhere, a sound of time standing still in one small flat while the city outside changes completely.","The film was a major hit in Germany and abroad and won the European Film Award for best film. The score was praised for avoiding both irony and sentimentality, though some listeners felt it repeated the Amélie formula in a sadder key. It has proved one of Tiersen's most durable soundtracks, and its piano pieces circulate widely in student repertoire.","Hear the main theme on solo piano, then the full string version, and notice how the second adds weight without adding melody. Then compare with Amélie: the same composer and similar devices, put to the service of loss rather than delight. A listener who likes this should next try Max Richter's On the Nature of Daylight."],"umebayashi-in-the-mood-for-love":["Wong Kar-wai's film about two neighbours in 1962 Hong Kong who discover their spouses are having an affair and almost fall in love themselves. Its music is remembered above all for Yumeji's Theme: a slow, dragging waltz in which plucked strings tread heavily on every beat while a solo violin sings a long, yearning line above them. It accompanies the film's slow-motion walks to a noodle stall, cheongsams swaying. Around it Wong placed Latin songs sung by Nat King Cole.","Yumeji's Theme was not written for this film. Umebayashi composed it for Seijun Suzuki's Yumeji of 1991, and Wong borrowed it, as he often borrowed music, building whole sequences around a pre-existing piece. Wong worked without a finished script, shaping scenes in the edit for more than a year, and the theme's insistent waltz rhythm became the pulse of the edit. The film premiered at Cannes in 2000.","The theme returns many times, nearly always over the two lovers passing each other in narrow corridors and stairwells, and almost never over dialogue. It becomes a ritual: the waltz is the sound of what they will not say. Listen to how the plucked bass drags just behind the beat, making the dance feel reluctant, as if each step had to be decided again.","The film is now regularly voted among the greatest of the century, and its music is inseparable from that reputation; Yumeji's Theme has been used in advertising and covered endlessly. Some purists note that the film's most famous music was written for another director, a reminder that film music's meaning lives in the cutting. Umebayashi's international career was largely launched by it.","Listen to Yumeji's Theme alone and then watch one of the slow-motion stairway sequences; the image and the waltz are made for each other. Then hear Nat King Cole's Quizás, quizás, quizás as Wong used it, a softer counter-melody. For a kindred obsession with longing in waltz time, try Badalamenti's music for Twin Peaks, and for Umebayashi's grander side, his House of Flying Daggers."],"umebayashi-house-of-flying-daggers":["Umebayashi's score for Zhang Yimou's wuxia romance, set in the waning Tang dynasty, about a blind dancer, two soldiers and a rebel sisterhood. The music blends a large Western string orchestra with Chinese instruments, the pipa's plucked tremolo and the bowed erhu, and builds lush, sorrowful love themes over sweeping bamboo-forest battles. It closes with a song, Lovers, sung in English by the American soprano Kathleen Battle.","Zhang had become the leading name in Chinese cinema's turn to lavish martial-arts epics after Hero in 2002, and House of Flying Daggers was a big-budget international production aimed at audiences worldwide. Umebayashi, already known in the West through In the Mood for Love, was asked for a score that could carry pan-Asian romance on a Hollywood scale. The film was released in 2004, opening out of competition at Cannes.","In the Echo Game scene, the dancer strikes drums arranged in a ring as pebbles are flicked at them, and that rhythmic sequence is part of the score's design: percussion that is also choreography. Listen for how Umebayashi saves the full orchestra for emotional rather than physical climaxes; many of the fights are scored lightly, and the big swells come in the moments of betrayal.","The film's visual design drew most of the praise, while the score divided critics: some heard a heady romantic sweep, others a generic East-meets-West wash. The Kathleen Battle song is either a coup or a miscalculation depending on whom you ask. Among film-music admirers the love theme has kept a steady following, and the score shows how far Umebayashi's palette reaches beyond his famous waltz.","Start with the main love theme, then the Echo Game sequence for its percussion. Compare it with Tan Dun's score for Hero and Ang Lee's Crouching Tiger to hear three takes on Chinese martial-arts romance. Then return to In the Mood for Love to hear how much smaller and stranger Umebayashi can be."],"cliff-martinez-solaris":["Steven Soderbergh's remake of Stanisław Lem's novel follows a psychologist on a space station orbiting an ocean planet that resurrects the dead. Martinez's score is a slow, glistening tide: shimmering metallic tones, soft bell-like patterns that cycle without a downbeat, and string pads that swell and recede. Its signature sound is the Cristal Baschet, a French instrument of glass rods rubbed with wet fingers, which gives a ringing, almost vocal shimmer that seems to have no attack at all.","Martinez, a former rock drummer, had been Soderbergh's regular composer since sex, lies, and videotape in 1989. For Solaris, a love story disguised as science fiction, Soderbergh wanted something unlike either Hollywood space music or Eduard Artemyev's electronic score for Tarkovsky's 1972 version. Martinez, a collector of unusual instruments, built the sound around his Baschet and electronic processing, recording much of it himself.","The music rarely sits on a strong beat; repeating patterns in different lengths drift in and out of alignment, so the score feels like it is breathing rather than counting. Listen for the moments when an ordinary string chord appears among the metallic shimmer: they tend to arrive with memories of earth and the dead wife, a small human warmth inside the alien sound.","The film was a box-office disappointment and divided critics, but the score acquired a cult following among electronic and ambient listeners and is often counted among the best film music of its decade. It is cited as a forerunner of the textural, pulse-led film scoring that became common in the 2010s, and Martinez's Baschet sound has been widely imitated.","Play it in the dark on good headphones and try to locate where each sound is coming from. Then hear Brian Eno's Music for Airports for the ambient lineage, and Ligeti's Atmosphères, which an earlier generation of space films used for the same sense of floating. Follow with Martinez's Drive to hear his synth-pulse side."],"cliff-martinez-drive":["Music for Nicolas Winding Refn's neo-noir about a nameless Los Angeles stunt driver who moonlights as a getaway man. Martinez's score is cold and patient: pulsing analogue-style synthesizers, sustained pads, distant arpeggios that tick like an idling engine, and sudden menacing drones when violence erupts. The soundtrack album mixes these cues with retro synth-pop songs by other artists, and together they create the film's nocturnal, neon-lit mood of glamour and dread.","Refn wanted the film to feel like a 1980s fairy tale, and the music helped place it there, drawing on Tangerine Dream and Giorgio Moroder rather than conventional thriller writing. The film premiered at Cannes in 2011, where Refn won the directing prize. Martinez's contribution was the instrumental score, around and between the licensed songs. The soundtrack album became an unexpected bestseller and a touchstone for the synthwave movement that followed.","Many of Martinez's cues barely move: a single chord held for a minute while a quiet pulse marks time, so that when the violence comes the music often does not react but simply continues, cool and indifferent. That refusal to underline the action is the score's real originality. Listen for the soft high arpeggio in the night-driving scenes, which is never quite in time with the visible cars.","Critics praised the soundtrack as central to the film's style, and it has outlasted many contemporaries in influence; its retro synth palette was adopted by countless films, television series and video games in the following decade. Some argue the songs did the heavy lifting and the score only supplies atmosphere; others hear Martinez's restraint as the hidden structure that lets the songs land.","Listen to the score cues on their own first, then the album in sequence, and notice how the songs and cues share tempo and colour. Pair it with Vangelis's Blade Runner and Tangerine Dream's Phaedra for the lineage, and with Daft Punk's Tron: Legacy for a parallel revival. Martinez's earlier Solaris shows the same patience in a different universe."],"johannsson-the-theory-of-everything":["Jóhann Jóhannsson's score for James Marsh's film about Stephen Hawking and his first wife Jane, from their meeting in Cambridge through his diagnosis with motor neurone disease and his growing fame. The best-known cue is a rippling, repeating piano figure that rises with strings into a bright, accelerating whirl, like a mind in motion. Around it sit tender, hymn-like string pieces and quiet electronic textures. It is the most openly melodic of Jóhannsson's film scores.","Jóhannsson had come to film from Icelandic experimental and post-minimalist albums, and after Prisoners in 2013 this was his first large mainstream drama. The film was a British prestige production, and its score won the Golden Globe and earned Oscar and BAFTA nominations. Eddie Redmayne won the Academy Award for playing Hawking. The success placed Jóhannsson among the most sought-after composers in cinema, leading directly to his work with Denis Villeneuve.","The music most associated with Hawking's thought is built from simple repeating patterns that layer and accelerate, a minimalist technique borrowed from Philip Glass and Michael Nyman, used here to suggest an idea gathering speed. Listen for how the score turns the device backwards near the end, as the film rewinds through the couple's life: the same figures, now heard as memory rather than momentum.","The score was widely praised, though some critics found it more conventional than Jóhannsson's own albums, the warmth of a prestige biopic rather than the strangeness of his best work. Its piano cue became a staple of classical streaming playlists. After his death in 2018 it is often heard as the bridge between his experimental early records and the darker Villeneuve scores.","Start with the title cue, then hear one of the slow string pieces to feel the contrast between motion and stillness. Compare with Glass's The Hours, which uses repeating patterns to portray a mind under pressure. Then listen to Jóhannsson's Sicario, made a year later, to hear how far he could travel from this warmth."],"johannsson-sicario":["Music for Denis Villeneuve's thriller about an FBI agent drawn into a secretive American operation against a Mexican cartel. Almost melody-free, the score is built from dread: enormous low brass and string tones that grind slowly upward, a heavy drum pattern like a heartbeat or approaching footsteps, and scraping, distorted textures. The cue called The Beast, for a convoy crossing into Juárez, is the one listeners remember: a vast, pulsing descent into menace.","This was Jóhannsson's first collaboration with Villeneuve after Prisoners, and it immediately followed The Theory of Everything, so the change of manner was abrupt. He wanted the score to feel like a physical pressure, the sound of the border itself. Hildur Guðnadóttir, his close collaborator, played cello on the score. It was nominated for the Academy Award, and when the sequel was made in 2018, after Jóhannsson's death, Guðnadóttir wrote its music.","The drum pattern that runs through the score is not quite regular: Jóhannsson lets it lurch, so the body never settles into it. Listen too for how much of the low sound is real orchestra rather than synthesis, heavily layered and slowed, a technique that keeps the menace oddly human. The cello lines are where the score's few moments of grief live.","Critics singled out the score as one of the film's principal achievements and it was widely imitated; the low, pulsing dread of The Beast became a template for thrillers and trailers in the late 2010s. Some argue that its influence flattened the genre into endless drones, a charge that falls more on the imitators than on the original. It is now regarded as Jóhannsson's defining dark score.","Listen to The Beast on good speakers or headphones, at volume, and feel rather than follow it. Then hear Guðnadóttir's Joker to trace how her cello writing grew out of this partnership. Penderecki's Threnody for the Victims of Hiroshima shows where the method of massed, grinding strings came from."],"johannsson-mandy":["Jóhannsson's score for Panos Cosmatos's hallucinatory revenge film, set in the Pacific Northwest in 1983, in which a lumberjack, played by Nicolas Cage, hunts the cult that destroyed his life. The music is heavy and slow: distorted electric guitars stretched into vast drones, synthesizers that recall early-1980s horror soundtracks, and passages of fragile, glowing tenderness for the couple's life before the catastrophe. It sounds like doom metal played in a cathedral.","Mandy premiered at the Sundance Film Festival in January 2018, a few weeks before Jóhannsson's death in Berlin that February at forty-eight. It was among the last of his scores released, and the final one heard in a film during his lifetime. Cosmatos wanted a sound rooted in heavy rock and the synthesizer music of his childhood, which drew Jóhannsson far from the orchestral prestige of his Oscar-nominated work.","Beneath the guitar weight, the score keeps returning to a simple, slow chord progression that belongs to the lovers; as the film descends into violence, the same progression comes back distorted and buried, so the tenderness is still audible inside the rage. Listen for the early-synth arpeggios that are deliberately period-correct, part of the film's dream of a lost 1983.","The film became a cult success, and the score was praised as a bold late turn, proof that Jóhannsson was still reinventing himself. For many listeners it is the most moving of his records because it is the last, and reviews were coloured by grief. Some critics find it overwhelming for its length; admirers hear that excess as precisely the point.","Play the opening cues loud and let the guitars become texture rather than riffs. Then compare with Sunn O)))'s Monoliths and Dimensions for drone metal at its source, and with Tangerine Dream's 1970s records for the synthesizer lineage. Return afterwards to the tender cues, which sound different once you know what follows them."],"goransson-black-panther":["Ludwig Göransson's score for Ryan Coogler's Marvel film about the king of Wakanda, a hidden, technologically advanced African nation. It fuses three worlds: a full Hollywood orchestra, West African and southern African instruments and voices, and hip-hop drum programming. The talking drum, whose pitch bends under the player's arm, announces T'Challa; a breathy Fula flute, with the player's voice audible inside the tone, haunts the villain Killmonger, whose cues also carry heavy 808 bass from American hip-hop.","Göransson had scored Coogler's Fruitvale Station and Creed and wanted Black Panther to avoid a generic African pastiche. He travelled to Senegal, touring with the singer Baaba Maal, and spent time in South Africa recording musicians and studying rhythm and song before writing for orchestra in Los Angeles. A separate companion album of songs curated by Kendrick Lamar was released alongside. The score won the Academy Award for Best Original Score.","Killmonger's theme begins on the Fula flute and then, as the film reveals he grew up in Oakland, is answered by hip-hop drums and bass, so his music literally carries his divided heritage. Listen in the waterfall challenge scenes for the chanted voices and drums recorded in Africa, layered with the orchestra rather than placed on top of it as decoration.","The score was hailed as a breakthrough for superhero music, a genre often criticised for anonymous orchestral wash, and its Oscar was the first for a Marvel film score. Some critics noted that the orchestral writing is more conventional than the African material, and the balance between authenticity and blockbuster convention is still discussed. Its integration of hip-hop production into orchestral scoring was widely imitated.","Start with the Wakanda cue for the talking drum and the main theme, then with Killmonger's theme for the flute-into-808 transformation. Hear Baaba Maal and Fela Kuti for the traditions Göransson studied, and Kendrick Lamar's companion album for the hip-hop side. Then compare with Zimmer's The Lion King, an earlier Hollywood take on African sound."],"goransson-the-mandalorian":["The score for the first live-action Star Wars television series, a lone bounty hunter's western-style odyssey across the galaxy. The main theme opens with a breathy, low wooden melody played on a bass recorder, before electronic drums, a twanging guitar and a full orchestra pile in. It sounds less like John Williams than like Ennio Morricone's spaghetti westerns crossed with 1980s synthesizer music, with a touch of samurai film thrown in.","Jon Favreau commissioned the series for the launch of Disney's streaming service in 2019, and the decision to avoid the Williams sound was deliberate: this was a story set at the edges of the Star Wars world. Göransson, by then an Oscar winner for Black Panther, played the recorder himself and built many cues in his studio before orchestrating them. The music won an Emmy and became one of the most recognisable television themes of its era.","The bass recorder, an instrument mostly associated with Renaissance consorts and school music lessons, is the quiet joke of the theme: a lonely, slightly comic sound standing in for a hard bounty hunter. Listen for how the theme's drum-machine rhythm and its western guitar keep appearing in fragments, while the Williams themes of the films are almost never quoted.","Fans and critics praised the score for giving the series its own identity, though some purists missed the Williams leitmotifs. It helped establish that streaming television could support ambitious, theme-driven orchestral music, and Göransson's success here contributed to a wider shift towards producer-composers working across pop and orchestra.","Hear the main title, then compare with Morricone's The Good, the Bad and the Ugly for the western ancestry and Williams's Star Wars main title for what it refuses. Then listen to Göransson's Oppenheimer to hear how differently he can use a solo instrument."],"goransson-oppenheimer":["Ludwig Göransson's score for Christopher Nolan's biography of J. Robert Oppenheimer, the physicist who led the Manhattan Project. The score begins on a solo violin, nervous and fluttering, which Göransson conceived as the sound of Oppenheimer's restless mind, and gradually multiplies it into a huge, churning string orchestra with pulsing synthesizers underneath. The music rarely settles; it races, swells and spirals, and runs under much of the three-hour film almost without stopping.","This was Göransson's second film with Nolan, after Tenet in 2020. Nolan wanted the score to carry the speed of thought, and Göransson began with the violin as a precise, vulnerable instrument whose vibrato could turn from romantic to neurotic. The film became an unexpected box-office giant in 2023, and the score won the Academy Award, the Golden Globe and the BAFTA.","The film's most famous sequence, the Trinity test, is not where the score explodes: Nolan and Göransson cut almost to silence at the moment of detonation, so the light arrives without sound and the shock wave comes much later. Listen for how the violin theme grows denser and faster over the film, its notes speeding up as the bomb gets closer.","The score was a critical and popular success, with its main cues widely streamed. Some reviewers found its near-constant presence exhausting, arguing that a three-hour drama needed more breathing space; admirers answer that the music is the film's engine, the anxiety it never lets you escape. It confirmed Göransson as one of the most decorated composers of his generation.","Start with the violin cue called Can You Hear the Music, then listen to how it returns at full orchestral weight. Then hear Zimmer's Interstellar, another Nolan score, for a contrasting organ-led approach. Penderecki's Threnody and Ligeti's Atmosphères show older ways of sounding nuclear-age terror."],"britell-moonlight":["Nicholas Britell's music for Barry Jenkins's three-part film about Chiron, a gay Black boy growing up in Miami, seen as a child, a teenager and an adult. The principal theme, Little's Theme, is a simple, tender melody for violin and piano, almost a lullaby. As Chiron grows, the same material is slowed down and lowered in pitch, darkened and thickened until the violins sound like cellos and the music seems to sink under its own weight.","Moonlight was a small independent film that won the Academy Award for Best Picture in 2017, in the famous ceremony at which the wrong winner was first announced. Jenkins and Britell discussed how the music should mirror the hip-hop culture of the film's world, specifically the chopped and screwed style pioneered in Houston by DJ Screw, in which records are slowed and pitched down. Britell applied that technique to his own orchestral recordings. The score was Oscar-nominated.","The chopped and screwed technique means you are often hearing the same recording at different speeds: the childhood violin theme, played back much slower and deeper, becomes the adult Black's theme. Listen for the moment in the final chapter where the music seems to have no bottom; it is the child's tune, still there, buried beneath a grown man's armour.","The score was widely praised as one of the most original of its decade, a classical-trained composer adopting a Southern hip-hop production technique as a structural principle rather than a gesture. It made Britell's name. Some listeners find the main theme too sweet on its own; within the film, its sweetness is precisely what the later chapters bury.","Hear Little's Theme first, then the later versions of it, and listen for the change in speed and depth. Then try a chopped and screwed track from the Houston scene to hear the source technique, and UGK's Ridin' Dirty for the wider Texas sound. Britell's Succession theme shows the same marriage of classical writing and hip-hop low end."],"britell-succession":["The score for Jesse Armstrong's HBO drama about a media dynasty whose ageing patriarch toys with his grasping children. The main title is the series in miniature: a slightly out-of-tune piano plays a stately, classical-sounding theme, strings and brass swell into grandeur, and underneath it all thumps a heavy hip-hop beat with deep bass. Through four seasons Britell spun dozens of variations on this music, from ornate baroque-style pastiches to stark piano solos.","Britell had followed Moonlight with If Beale Street Could Talk and was building a reputation for unusual juxtapositions. For Succession he wanted music that sounded like old money, classical and imperious, but also undercut and corrupted, which led him to the eighteenth-century pastiche and the modern bass. The series ran from 2018 to 2023, and the theme won Emmys and became a popular phenomenon, remixed by the rapper Pusha T.","The piano in the main title is deliberately slightly detuned, a crack in the varnish of wealth; listen for the faint clash on the upper notes. Britell's many variations also borrow the forms of classical music, minuets, chaconnes and grand marches, giving the Roy family a court composer who quietly mocks them.","The theme became one of the most recognisable of its era, and the score as a whole is praised for doing comic and tragic work at once. Some critics noted the music's role in making monstrous characters oddly compelling. It is cited as a model of how prestige television developed a musical identity as distinctive as cinema's.","Start with the main title and listen for the bass beneath the grand piano theme. Then hear one of the baroque-style variations and compare it with the Bach or Handel it imitates. Finish with Britell's Moonlight to hear his first experiment in marrying classical writing to hip-hop production."],"hurwitz-la-la-land":["Justin Hurwitz's music for Damien Chazelle's original film musical about a jazz pianist and an actress chasing success in Los Angeles. Songs and score are woven from a few melodies: the soft, wistful duet City of Stars; the brassy freeway number Another Day of Sun; and Mia and Sebastian's Theme, a simple piano melody that returns throughout. The closing sequence, an extended wordless fantasy, replays the whole score as a ballet of what might have been.","Hurwitz and Chazelle met as students at Harvard and had been planning a musical since their student film Guy and Madeline on a Park Bench. Their first commercial success, Whiplash, was about jazz drumming; La La Land was their tribute to Jacques Demy's sung films and the Hollywood musical. The song lyrics are by Benj Pasek and Justin Paul. The film won six Oscars, including Hurwitz's for score and for City of Stars.","The epilogue quotes nearly every theme in the film in a new order, reimagining the couple's story as it could have been; listen for how the piano theme, until then incomplete, is finally allowed to run its full course. Demy's Les Parapluies de Cherbourg is the clear model, down to the ending of separated lovers.","The film was a huge popular success, but its depiction of jazz, in which a white pianist sets out to save the art form, drew sharp criticism. Many jazz critics found the music's idea of the idiom nostalgic and narrow. The orchestral writing is generally praised for its craft and lyricism; its debt to Demy and to Michel Legrand is acknowledged openly.","Hear Mia and Sebastian's Theme first, then the epilogue, where everything returns. Then watch or hear Legrand's Les Parapluies de Cherbourg, the film's acknowledged ancestor. For contrast, try Bill Evans's Peace Piece to hear the kind of jazz piano the story idealises."],"hurwitz-first-man":["Hurwitz's score for Damien Chazelle's film about Neil Armstrong and the long, deadly road to Apollo 11. It balances two worlds: tender, intimate music for the family, centred on harp and strings, and huge, rising orchestral and electronic cues for the space flights. The theremin, an early electronic instrument played without touch, sings a ghostly melody through both, and the Moon landing is marked by a long orchestral swell in waltz time that seems to float.","This was Hurwitz's third feature with Chazelle, after Whiplash and La La Land. The film was released in 2018, and the score won the Golden Globe. Chazelle wanted the film to feel physical and frightening rather than heroic, so the score stays quiet in much of the cramped capsule footage and saves its expansion for the Moon. The theremin connects to a real detail of Armstrong's life.","Armstrong took a recording of Music Out of the Moon, a 1947 album of theremin mood pieces, with him on the Apollo 11 mission, and Hurwitz's use of the theremin, especially in the waltz-time Lunar Rhapsody, quietly honours it. Listen for the theremin entering not in the spacecraft but in a domestic scene, linking home and the Moon.","The film was admired but underperformed commercially, and the score was praised for being Hurwitz's most ambitious. Some critics noted the waltz as a deliberate nod to Kubrick's use of The Blue Danube in 2001, others heard it as its own invention. It is regarded by many film-music listeners as Hurwitz's finest work.","Start with The Landing for the long orchestral swell, then the Lunar Rhapsody for the theremin, and notice how the instrument's wavering voice sounds both nostalgic and eerie. Compare with Richard Strauss's Also sprach Zarathustra, the music Kubrick sent into space, and with Zimmer's Interstellar for another score balancing family and the cosmos. Brian Eno's Apollo is the ambient view of the same missions."],"danna-the-sweet-hereafter":["Mychael Danna's score for Atom Egoyan's film about a small town in British Columbia after a school-bus crash kills most of its children, and the lawyer who arrives to sue on their behalf. The music has an archaic, otherworldly sound, using instruments with the colour of early-music consorts, and recurring melodies with the shape of a folk tune or a fairy-tale lament. It moves slowly and sparsely, as quiet as the snow over the town.","Danna had scored every Egoyan feature since Family Viewing in 1987, and their partnership was central to the Canadian cinema of the 1990s. The film, adapted from Russell Banks's novel, is threaded with Robert Browning's poem The Pied Piper of Hamelin, read to children by a teenage babysitter. That story of a whole town's children led away gave Danna the cue for his medieval-sounding instruments. The film won the Grand Prix at Cannes in 1997.","Danna's archaic sound is tied directly to the Pied Piper: the music is meant to recall the piper's tune that led Hamelin's children away, so every recurrence of the melody carries the memory of the bus. Listen for how rarely the score is used under dialogue, and how often it enters just as the camera pulls away from a face into the snowy landscape.","The film is widely considered Egoyan's finest and among the great Canadian films, and the score was praised for its strange restraint. Some listeners find the medieval colouring an affectation; admirers argue it supplies the fairy-tale distance that makes unbearable grief watchable. It is often named as Danna's most personal work.","Hear the main theme alone, then imagine it as the piper's tune: the film's logic becomes clear. Pair it with Dowland's Lachrimae Pavan for real early music in the same mood, and with Delerue's Black Robe for another winter landscape score. Danna's Life of Pi shows his later, much larger world-music palette."],"danna-life-of-pi":["Danna's score for Ang Lee's film of Yann Martel's novel, about an Indian boy adrift in a lifeboat with a Bengal tiger. It begins with Pi's Lullaby, sung in Tamil over a gently rocking accompaniment, and moves between South Indian instruments and voices, a large Western orchestra, and ethereal choral and electronic textures for the ocean. The score's arc follows Pi from Pondicherry's colourful world through the terrors and wonders of the sea.","Danna had a long interest in Indian music, having worked in India on several films, including Mira Nair's Monsoon Wedding. Ang Lee wanted the film's spiritual openness, Pi practising Hinduism, Christianity and Islam at once, mirrored by a score that blended traditions. Life of Pi was released in 2012 in 3D, and Danna won both the Academy Award and the Golden Globe. Pi's Lullaby, sung by the Carnatic vocalist Bombay Jayashri, was Oscar-nominated as a song.","The lullaby returns in many forms across the film, from voice and strings to wordless orchestral surges, binding the shipwrecked boy to his mother's voice. Listen for the cue that accompanies the luminous sea at night, where Danna's textures dissolve almost entirely into shimmer, before the orchestra returns for the storm.","The score was widely admired for weaving Indian music into a Hollywood epic without treating it as exotic decoration, and its Oscar was considered well earned. Some critics felt the big orchestral passages occasionally slipped back into generic uplift, at odds with the delicacy of the lullaby. It remains Danna's most popular score and the one that brought his long cross-cultural work to a mass audience.","Begin with Pi's Lullaby and listen to how the voice floats over a rocking accompaniment, then follow the score through the shipwreck to the storm cues, where the orchestra takes over. Compare with Sakamoto's The Last Emperor and Hisaishi's Princess Mononoke for other scores that fold Asian traditions into a large orchestra. For Carnatic singing at its source, seek out Bombay Jayashri's concert recordings."],"burwell-fargo":["Carter Burwell's score for the Coen brothers' dark comedy about a botched kidnapping in snowbound Minnesota and North Dakota. The main theme is grand and doleful, a slow, folk-like melody for strings and harp swelling over thudding drums, as a car crawls through a white-out. The score is short and sparing, but this theme returns in many guises, from a stately march to a quiet harp line, framing the film's mixture of comedy and violence.","Burwell had scored every Coen film since Blood Simple in 1984, and their collaboration had a playful sense of genre. Fargo, released in 1996, mixed crime thriller with deadpan Scandinavian-American Midwestern culture, and Burwell drew on that ancestry. The film won Oscars for its screenplay and for Frances McDormand, and became one of the most admired American films of its decade.","The main theme is based on a Norwegian folk melody, The Lost Sheep, a nod to the Scandinavian heritage of the region's settlers. Burwell turns its plain tune into something mock-epic: the grandeur is a little too grand for a story of small-time crooks, and the joke is part of the music's charm. Listen for it slowed to a crawl in the final scenes.","The theme became one of the most recognisable in 1990s film music, and has been widely used and parodied. Critics praised Burwell's sly, folk-tinged gravity, which fits the Coens' ambivalence between mockery and compassion. The later television series took a different musical approach, but the film's theme remains the one people remember.","Hear the main title, then the quieter harp version, and notice how the same tune can be both solemn and absurd. Compare with Grieg's Peer Gynt for Norwegian folk colour in orchestral form, and with Morricone's westerns for genre-scale melody applied to small lives. Then hear Burwell's Carol for a very different side of him."],"burwell-carol":["Burwell's score for Todd Haynes's film of Patricia Highsmith's novel about a love affair between a young shop assistant and an older married woman in New York in the early 1950s. The music is soft and circling: repeating clarinet and string figures, gentle piano chords, and long, yearning melodies that rise and fall like held breath. It captures both the thrill and the danger of a forbidden relationship, conveyed mostly through restraint.","Haynes had made several films about mid-century America, and Carol, released in 2015, was a lush period piece starring Cate Blanchett and Rooney Mara. Burwell, best known for his Coen brothers scores, wrote music inspired by repetitive minimalist textures to suggest the passage of time and the obsessive inner life of the characters. The score was nominated for the Academy Award.","The repeating figures in the score keep shifting slightly, never quite locking into a steady loop, which reflects the instability of the women's relationship and the society that forbids it. Listen for the moment when the main theme is played by full strings for the first time, after many partial statements: the musical equivalent of a long-delayed confession.","The film was acclaimed, and Burwell's score was praised as one of his most emotionally direct. Some critics noted the debt to Philip Glass's repetitive textures, while others heard Burwell's own voice in the harmonies. The score is often counted among the best of the 2010s.","Start with the opening cue, listening for the clarinets, then the main love theme in full. Compare with Glass's The Hours, another score of repetition for women's inner lives, and with Thomas Newman's American Beauty. Then return to Burwell's Fargo to hear his range."],"portman-emma":["Rachel Portman's score for Douglas McGrath's film of Jane Austen's novel, about a young woman in Regency England who meddles in her friends' romances. The music is light and graceful, written for a small orchestra: lilting strings, playful woodwinds, a harp and piano, and melodies that dance gently in the manner of a country ball. Its main theme is sunny and wistful at once, and its textures are as transparent as the comedy of manners it accompanies.","The mid-1990s saw a boom in Austen adaptations, and Emma, released in 1996 with Gwyneth Paltrow in the title role, was one of the most successful. Portman had built a career in British film and television, and this score won her the Academy Award for Best Original Musical or Comedy Score. She was the first woman to win an Oscar for film scoring, a milestone in a field dominated by men.","Portman avoids pastiche of Regency music, writing instead in her own modern lyrical idiom, but she borrows the lightness and dance rhythms of the period. Listen for the woodwinds, which often play little comic commentaries on Emma's schemes, a musical equivalent of Austen's ironic narrator.","The score was well-received, and its Oscar was historic, though some critics found it charming but lightweight compared with more ambitious scores of the year. Its significance has grown with time, as a landmark for women in film music, and Portman became one of the most respected composers in the field.","Hear the main theme first, then one of the comic cues, and notice how the woodwinds wink. Compare with Mozart's lighter serenades and with Thomas Newman's scores of the same era. Then try Portman's The Cider House Rules for her more melancholic side."],"portman-the-cider-house-rules":["Portman's score for Lasse Hallström's film of John Irving's novel, about an orphan raised in a Maine orphanage by a kindly, unorthodox doctor, who leaves to see the world. The music is warm and melancholy: a gentle, rocking waltz for piano and strings, expansive pastoral themes for the apple orchards, and tender lullaby-like music for the orphans. It is more emotionally weighted than her Austen scores, but retains the same clarity.","The film, released in 1999, was a prestige drama for Miramax and was nominated for Best Picture. Portman had followed Emma with a series of literary adaptations, and her score for this film earned an Oscar nomination. The story's themes of abortion and choice were controversial, and the score had to support a film balancing sentiment with moral complexity.","The main waltz is built on a rocking, repeating accompaniment that suggests both the motion of a cradle and the turning of the seasons, linking the orphanage to the orchards. Listen for how the theme returns at the end, now fuller and more resolved, as the boy returns home.","The score was praised as one of Portman's finest and earned her a second Academy Award nomination, with critics noting its emotional depth and restraint. Some found it sentimental, the same charge laid against Hallström's film and its careful softening of Irving's darker novel. It has nonetheless become one of her most popular works, its main theme frequently arranged for piano and played in concert.","Start with the main theme, a gently rocking waltz for piano and strings, then the broader orchard music, and listen for how little the orchestra needs to do to move you. Compare with Thomas Newman's The Shawshank Redemption for another American pastoral score of the 1990s, and with Copland's Appalachian Spring for the older tradition behind both. Then hear Emma for Portman's lighter manner."],"korzeniowski-a-single-man":["Abel Korzeniowski's score for Tom Ford's film of Christopher Isherwood's novel, about a gay English professor in Los Angeles in 1962, grieving his partner and planning to end his life. The music is lush and romantic, built on surging string melodies and rippling piano, often in a slow, dance-like pulse. Its signature cue is a soaring, aching string waltz that captures the professor's longing and beauty-obsessed despair.","Ford, the fashion designer, made his directing debut with this film in 2009, and its exquisite design was matched by the score's elegance. Korzeniowski, a Polish composer, was relatively unknown in Hollywood, and this score earned him a Golden Globe nomination and international attention. Some cues in the film were written by Shigeru Umebayashi, Wong Kar-wai's composer, whose influence Ford acknowledged.","The score is in constant dialogue with Bernard Herrmann's Vertigo, with its swirling strings and obsessive circling figures, and with Umebayashi's In the Mood for Love, whose waltz-like slow pulse it echoes. Listen for how the strings often play a melody slightly behind the beat, giving the music a reluctant, lingering quality.","The score was widely praised and has become a favourite among film-music listeners, with its main waltz performed in concert and used far beyond the film. Some critics found it overly lush and too close to its models, but most agreed that its heightened romanticism matched Ford's meticulous visual style perfectly. The Golden Globe nomination it earned launched Korzeniowski's international career.","Start with the main string waltz and listen for how the melody hangs back, then hear the quieter piano cues for their intimacy. Compare with Herrmann's Vertigo, the obvious ancestor, and with Umebayashi's In the Mood for Love, set in the very same year of 1962. Then try Korzeniowski's Penny Dreadful for a darker, gothic version of his romantic style."],"korzeniowski-penny-dreadful":["Korzeniowski's score for John Logan's Showtime horror series, set in Victorian London, gathering Dracula, Frankenstein's creature, Dorian Gray and other literary monsters. The main title is a jagged, insistent string ostinato that builds into a feverish dance, with a solo violin and choir rising above. The score moves between gothic dread, dark romance and religious ecstasy, using the same lush strings as A Single Man in a far darker register.","The series, which ran from 2014 to 2016, was a lavish gothic production starring Eva Green. Korzeniowski, building on his success with A Single Man and W.E., wrote for full orchestra and choir, giving the series a cinematic scale unusual for television at the time. The score won acclaim and a devoted following among genre fans.","The main title's ostinato is built on short, scraping string figures that repeat with small variations, suggesting both a heartbeat and the scuttling of something unseen. Listen for the choir, which often enters with wordless religious harmonies, a hint of the series' preoccupation with faith, damnation and possession.","The score was praised for its lushness and drama, and the main title is often counted among the best television themes of its decade. Some critics found it overheated, but most agreed it fitted the series' baroque excess. It cemented Korzeniowski's reputation for romantic, emotionally charged writing.","Start with the main title, then hear one of the romantic cues and one of the horror cues. Compare with Herrmann's Psycho for the scraping strings and with Penderecki's Threnody for the gothic horror lineage. Then return to A Single Man for the romantic side."],"kris-bowers-when-they-see-us":["Kris Bowers's score for Ava DuVernay's four-part Netflix drama about the five Black and Latino teenagers wrongly convicted in the 1989 Central Park jogger case. The music is restrained and haunting: sustained strings, quiet piano, ambient electronic textures, and slow, aching melodies. It avoids melodrama, letting the story's injustice speak for itself, and grows darker and more fragmented as the boys move through the prison system.","DuVernay's series, released in 2019, was a major event, prompting renewed debate about racial injustice in the American legal system. Bowers, a jazz pianist trained at Juilliard and winner of the Thelonious Monk International Jazz Piano Competition, had scored Green Book and other films and was emerging as one of the most versatile young composers in Hollywood. His work here was widely praised.","Bowers ties the score's melodic material to each of the five boys, so that the music's fragmentation mirrors their separation by the prison system. Listen for the piano, which carries much of the score's emotion, and for the moments when the strings swell only to break off, a musical image of hope repeatedly denied.","The series was acclaimed and widely discussed, and Bowers's score was praised for its restraint and its refusal to tell the audience what to feel. Some critics noted its debt to the minimalist and ambient film scores of the 2010s, while others heard a distinctive voice shaped by his jazz training. It confirmed Bowers as one of the leading young composers working in American screen music.","Start with the opening cues of the first episode, where the music is at its most open, then listen to a later prison cue and hear how the same piano material has thinned and fractured. Compare with Britell's Moonlight for another restrained score about Black lives in America, and with Reich's Different Trains for music that confronts historical injustice head on."],"pemberton-steve-jobs":["Daniel Pemberton's score for Danny Boyle's film, written by Aaron Sorkin, about Steve Jobs backstage before three product launches: the Macintosh in 1984, the NeXT computer in 1988 and the iMac in 1998. Pemberton gave each act its own sound world: buzzing, early analogue synthesizers for 1984; a full orchestra with operatic grandeur for 1988, the act staged in an opera house; and sleek, minimal digital electronics for 1998.","Boyle and Sorkin's film, released in 2015, was structured like a three-act play, and Pemberton saw the chance to make the score a portrait of technological change. A British composer with roots in television and electronic music, he wrote the 1984 music on vintage synths, the 1988 music for orchestra, and the 1998 music in a modern digital idiom. The score earned a Golden Globe nomination.","The three acts share melodic material, transformed by each technology: the same theme sounds crude and hopeful on 1984 synthesizers, grand and overbearing in the 1988 orchestra, and cool and polished in 1998. Listen for how the music itself narrates the changing face of computing and Jobs's own evolution.","The film was praised for its performances and Sorkin's script but disappointed at the box office, and Pemberton's score was singled out for its conceptual daring. Some critics found the three-part structure schematic, a clever idea more admired than felt; most admired its ingenuity and the way it gave a talky film momentum. It established Pemberton as one of the most inventive composers in British film.","Hear one cue from each of the three acts in order, and listen for how the same ideas change their clothes as the technology changes. Compare with Wendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach for the early synthesizer sound of the first act and with Glass's Koyaanisqatsi for a score about machines and modern life. Then try Pemberton's Spider-Verse for his playful side."],"pemberton-spider-man-into-the-spider-verse":["Pemberton's score for the animated film about Miles Morales, a Brooklyn teenager who becomes Spider-Man and meets alternate Spider-People from other dimensions. The music fuses a heroic orchestra with hip-hop beats, turntable scratching, glitchy electronics and record-skip effects, so the score sounds as if it were being remixed as you hear it. Each Spider-Person gets their own sonic identity, from noir jazz to anime-style synths.","The film, released in 2018, was a stylistic breakthrough in animation, mimicking comic-book printing and frame rates, and it won the Academy Award for Best Animated Feature. Pemberton matched its visual innovation with a score that drew on the hip-hop culture of Miles's world. The film's companion album of songs, including Post Malone and Swae Lee's Sunflower, was a huge hit.","Pemberton uses scratching and record-skip effects not merely as colour but as structure: the score literally stutters and glitches when dimensions collide, as if the film itself were a record being cued up by a DJ. Listen for how Miles's theme grows from tentative scratches into a full orchestral and hip-hop anthem as he gains confidence, ending with the leap of faith.","The score was praised for its inventiveness, and the film is widely ranked among the best animated features ever made. Some critics felt the hit songs overshadowed Pemberton's work, but most admired how score and songs shared tempo, colour and attitude, so that the boundary between them blurs. It confirmed Pemberton as one of the most adventurous genre-blenders in film, and led to the sequel.","Start with Miles's theme and follow it from its hesitant scratches to its full anthem, then hear the cues for the alternate Spider-People and their contrasting styles. Compare with Göransson's Black Panther for another fusion of orchestra and hip-hop, and with Wu-Tang Clan's Enter the Wu-Tang for the New York hip-hop the film's world grows from. J Dilla's Donuts shows the art of the glitch."]});